/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/sc-500.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("sc-500", [["az1",0,1,"An engineer needs to give a helpdesk team the ability to reset passwords for non-admin users in Microsoft Entra ID, and nothing else. Which role should be assigned?",["User Administrator","Helpdesk Administrator","Global Reader","Privileged Authentication Administrator"],1,"Helpdesk Administrator can reset passwords for non-administrators and some limited admin roles. User Administrator also manages users and groups, which is more than required.","1: Entra roles",["User Administrator can create, delete and manage all non-admin users and groups, far more than the password-reset-only scope requested, breaking least privilege.",null,"Global Reader is read-only across the tenant and cannot reset any passwords.","Privileged Authentication Administrator can reset credentials and MFA for administrators too, a much broader and more sensitive scope than non-admin resets."],2],["az2",0,1,"Admins hold standing Owner rights on production subscriptions. The security team wants them to request Owner only when needed, with MFA and a written reason, for at most four hours. What should be configured?",["An Azure Policy Deny assignment on the Owner role","A Conditional Access policy for the Azure portal app","A custom role with fewer actions than Owner","A PIM eligible assignment with activation settings"],3,"PIM for Azure resources turns Owner into an eligible role that must be activated with MFA and justification for a limited duration. Conditional Access controls sign-in, not standing role membership.","1: Privileged Identity Management",["Azure Policy governs resource configuration and cannot control role membership or turn Owner into a request-on-demand assignment.","Conditional Access enforces sign-in conditions like MFA and location but does not remove the standing Owner assignment itself.","A custom role reduces permissions, but they would still be held permanently rather than activated for a limited window with justification.",null],2],["az3",0,1,"Which statement about Azure RBAC role assignment inheritance is correct?",["Assignments at a management group are inherited by child subscriptions","Assignments at a resource group apply to the subscription above it","Resource-level assignments override deny assignments","Only Owner assignments are inherited downward"],0,"Azure RBAC assignments flow down the scope hierarchy: management group, subscription, resource group, resource. They never flow upward, and deny assignments take precedence over role assignments.","1: Azure RBAC",[null,"Assignments never flow upward, so a resource group assignment does not apply to its parent subscription.","Deny assignments always take precedence over role assignments, so resource-level assignments cannot override them.","All role assignments inherit downward, not only Owner."],1],["az4",0,1,"A web app running in App Service must read secrets from Key Vault without any credential stored in code or configuration. What should be used?",["A client secret saved in app settings","The Key Vault access key","A managed identity for the web app","A shared access signature"],2,"A managed identity gets tokens from Microsoft Entra automatically, and the vault grants that identity a role such as Key Vault Secrets User. A client secret in app settings is still a stored credential. Key Vault has no access key or SAS.","1: Managed identities",["A client secret in app settings is still a stored credential that can leak and must be rotated, which the requirement forbids.","Key Vault has no 'access key'; access is granted through RBAC or access policies, not a key.",null,"A SAS is a storage or service token, not a Key Vault access mechanism, and would itself be a stored credential."],1],["az5",0,1,"Five function apps need identical permissions to a storage account, and the identity must survive the apps being redeployed. Which identity type fits best?",["A system-assigned managed identity on each app","An app registration with a certificate credential","A user-assigned managed identity shared by the apps","A guest account invited into the tenant"],2,"A user-assigned managed identity is a standalone resource that can be attached to many apps and is not deleted with them. System-assigned identities are one per resource and deleted with it.","1: Managed identities",["System-assigned identities are one per resource and deleted with the app, so they neither survive redeploy as a shared identity nor share permissions across five apps.","An app registration with a certificate reintroduces a managed credential that must be stored and rotated.",null,"Guest accounts are for external human users, not app-to-service authentication."],3],["az6",0,1,"Users are consenting to third-party apps that request access to their mailbox. The security team wants user consent limited to verified publishers and low-impact permissions, with an admin reviewing other requests. What should they configure?",["User consent settings plus the admin consent workflow","Redirect URI restrictions on each app registration","A Conditional Access policy blocking unknown locations","A PIM access review of the Global Administrators"],0,"User consent settings can restrict consent to verified publishers and selected permissions, and the admin consent workflow routes other requests to reviewers. Redirect URIs do not control consent.","1: App registrations and consent",[null,"Redirect URIs only control where tokens are returned to an app, not what permissions users may consent to.","Conditional Access governs sign-in conditions such as location, not application consent.","A PIM access review evaluates admin role membership, which is unrelated to third-party app consent."],2],["az7",0,1,"What is created in a tenant when a multitenant application is first consented to there?",["A new app registration in the tenant","A user-assigned managed identity for the app","A guest user account for the publisher","A service principal (enterprise application)"],3,"Consent creates a service principal, shown as an enterprise application, representing the app in that tenant. The app registration exists only in the home tenant.","1: App registrations and consent",["The app registration exists only in the application's home tenant; consenting in another tenant does not create one there.","Consent does not create a managed identity for the application.","Consent does not create a guest user for the publisher.",null],1],["az8",0,1,"A company wants to require MFA for all users signing in to the Azure portal, except when they are on the corporate network. What should be built?",["Per-user MFA enabled for every account in the tenant, with app passwords","A Conditional Access policy for Azure management that excludes a trusted named location","Security defaults with the corporate IP ranges added as trusted IPs","An Azure Policy assignment that audits sign-ins from outside the office"],1,"Conditional Access can target the Azure management app and exclude a named location marked trusted. Security defaults cannot be customized with exclusions, and Azure Policy does not govern sign-in.","1: Conditional Access",["Per-user MFA is legacy, cannot exclude the corporate network, and app passwords weaken security.",null,"Security defaults are all-or-nothing and cannot be customized with location exclusions.","Azure Policy governs resource configuration and cannot enforce or exclude sign-in behavior."],2],["az9",0,1,"Before enforcing a new Conditional Access policy that blocks legacy authentication, an engineer wants to see which sign-ins it would affect without blocking anyone. What should they do?",["Assign it to an empty group","Set the policy to Report-only","Enable it and review the audit log","Create a PIM access review"],1,"Report-only mode evaluates the policy on every sign-in and logs the result without enforcing it. Enabling it would block users immediately, and an empty group shows no impact at all.","1: Conditional Access",["Assigning the policy to an empty group puts no one in scope, so it reveals nothing about the real impact.",null,"Enabling the policy enforces the block on legacy auth immediately, which is exactly what they want to avoid first.","A PIM access review evaluates role membership, not the impact of a Conditional Access policy."],1],["az10",0,1,"Administrators must use phishing-resistant sign-in. Which Conditional Access grant control enforces this most directly?",["Require the device to be marked compliant in Intune","Require an approved client app on mobile platforms","Require a password change on every sign-in","Require an authentication strength of phishing-resistant MFA"],3,"Authentication strengths let a policy require specific methods, such as FIDO2 security keys or Windows Hello for Business. A compliant device does not stop an attacker from phishing the credentials themselves.","1: Authentication methods",["A compliant device proves device health but does nothing to stop an attacker who phishes the user's credentials.","Requiring an approved client app enforces app protection on mobile but is not phishing-resistant authentication.","Forcing a password change every sign-in churns passwords without preventing phishing; passwords remain phishable.",null],2],["az11",0,1,"A Key Vault uses the access policy model. A user with Contributor on the vault has no access policy but can still read secrets after a few minutes. Why?",["Contributor can edit the vault's access policies and grant itself access","The Contributor role includes secret data actions by default","Soft delete exposes recently deleted secrets to all Contributors","Access policies are ignored for users who hold the Contributor role"],0,"Under access policies, anyone who can write the vault resource can edit its access policies. The RBAC permission model separates management from data access, so Contributor alone cannot read secrets.","1: Key Vault",[null,"Contributor is a control-plane role with no data actions, so it does not include secret read permissions by default.","Soft delete retains deleted objects for recovery; it does not grant anyone read access to live secrets.","Contributor is not exempt from access policies; it gains access only by editing those policies, not by bypassing them."],3],["az12",0,1,"An auditor requires that deleted keys in a Key Vault cannot be permanently removed by anyone before the retention period ends. What must be enabled?",["Soft delete only","A ReadOnly resource lock","Purge protection","Key rotation policy"],2,"Purge protection prevents permanent deletion of a vault or its objects while they are in the soft-deleted state, even by administrators. Soft delete alone still allows a purge.","1: Key Vault",["Soft delete retains deleted objects for a recovery window but still permits an early purge.","A ReadOnly lock blocks resource writes and would break normal vault use; it does not stop purging soft-deleted objects.",null,"A key rotation policy schedules new key versions and has nothing to do with preventing permanent deletion."],1],["az13",0,1,"A security engineer wants Azure to rotate a customer-managed key in Key Vault every 90 days automatically. What should be configured?",["A Logic App that deletes the old key","Purge protection on the vault","A key rotation policy on the key","An expiration date on the vault"],2,"Key Vault key rotation policies generate a new key version on a schedule and can notify before expiry. Services that use versionless key URIs pick up the new version automatically.","1: Key Vault",["A Logic App that deletes the old key is custom, does not generate a scheduled new version, and deleting an in-use key breaks services.","Purge protection prevents permanent deletion of soft-deleted objects; it does not rotate keys.",null,"A vault has no expiration that rotates keys; an expiry date on a key simply makes it unusable when reached."],1],["az14",0,1,"New storage accounts must never allow public blob access. Existing accounts should be reported but not changed. Which Azure Policy effect meets the requirement for new resources?",["Deny","Audit","AuditIfNotExists","Disabled"],0,"Deny blocks create or update requests that violate the rule, while existing resources are only evaluated and marked non-compliant. Audit would allow new non-compliant accounts.","1: Azure Policy",[null,"Audit only flags non-compliant accounts after the fact and would still allow new accounts with public access.","AuditIfNotExists evaluates based on a related resource's existence and still does not block creation of new accounts.","Disabled turns the policy off entirely, enforcing nothing."],1],["az15",0,1,"A policy must automatically add a diagnostic setting to every new Key Vault that is missing one. Which effect and prerequisite are needed?",["Modify, with no identity required on the assignment","Deny, with a CanNotDelete lock on each vault","Append, with a policy exemption for existing vaults","DeployIfNotExists, with a managed identity for remediation"],3,"DeployIfNotExists deploys a related resource when it is missing, and the assignment needs a managed identity with rights to deploy it. Modify changes properties of the resource itself, such as tags.","1: Azure Policy",["Modify changes properties on the evaluated resource (such as tags) and cannot deploy a separate diagnostic setting; remediation also requires an identity.","Deny would block creating the vault rather than adding the missing diagnostic setting.","Append adds fields to the request payload; it cannot deploy a diagnostic setting resource.",null],3],["az16",0,1,"Twenty related policy definitions must be assigned together to many subscriptions and tracked as one compliance score. What should be created?",["A resource lock","A policy initiative","A management group per definition","A custom RBAC role"],1,"An initiative (policy set) groups definitions so they can be assigned and reported together. Management groups are scopes, not groupings of policies.","1: Azure Policy",["A resource lock prevents delete or modify operations; it does not group policy definitions.",null,"Management groups are assignment scopes, not a way to bundle definitions into one compliance score.","A custom RBAC role grants permissions and has nothing to do with grouping policies."],1],["az17",0,1,"An Owner tries to delete a resource group and receives an error even though they have full permissions. What is the most likely cause?",["The Owner role lacks delete actions","The resource group has a CanNotDelete lock","A Conditional Access policy blocks deletions","PIM is enabled for the subscription"],1,"Locks apply to everyone, including Owners, until removed. Owner includes all actions, and Conditional Access evaluates sign-ins, not specific delete operations.","1: Governance",["Owner includes all actions, including delete, so a missing permission is not the cause.",null,"Conditional Access evaluates sign-ins, not individual resource delete operations.","PIM governs role activation and does not block an Owner from deleting a resource group."],1],["az18",0,1,"A ReadOnly lock is placed on a storage account. What unexpected side effect is common?",["Blob data is re-encrypted with a new Microsoft-managed key","Users can no longer sign in to the Azure portal at all","Blob reads through Entra RBAC are blocked on the data plane","Listing the account keys fails because it is a POST operation"],3,"ReadOnly blocks all write and POST management operations, and listing keys is a POST, so tools that fetch keys break. Data-plane access through Entra RBAC is not blocked by the lock.","1: Governance",["A resource lock does not trigger re-encryption of blob data.","A resource lock does not affect a user's ability to sign in to the Azure portal.","ReadOnly restricts management-plane writes, not data-plane blob reads through Entra RBAC.",null],3],["az19",0,1,"A team needs a role that can restart VMs and read their configuration but cannot create or delete them. No built-in role matches. What should be created?",["A custom Azure role with the needed actions","A Microsoft Entra administrative unit","A PIM approval workflow","An Azure Policy initiative"],0,"A custom role lists exactly the allowed actions, such as Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/read and restart/action, with assignable scopes. Administrative units scope Entra directory roles, not Azure resource actions.","1: Azure RBAC",[null,"An administrative unit scopes Entra directory roles, not Azure resource actions such as restarting a VM.","A PIM approval workflow governs activation of an existing role; it does not create a new permission set.","An Azure Policy initiative enforces compliance and does not grant restart permissions."],1],["az20",0,1,"Which field in a custom Azure role definition grants permission to read blob contents rather than manage the storage account?",["actions","notActions","dataActions","assignableScopes"],2,"dataActions cover data-plane operations such as reading blobs or queue messages. actions cover control-plane operations on the resource itself.","1: Azure RBAC",["actions cover control-plane operations on the resource itself, not reading blob contents.","notActions subtracts control-plane permissions and does not grant data-plane access.",null,"assignableScopes defines where the role can be assigned, not what data it can access."],1],["az21",0,1,"The CISO wants guest users with access to production resource groups reviewed by resource owners every quarter, with access removed if not approved. What should be used?",["Azure Policy compliance reports","Defender for Cloud secure score","Microsoft Entra access reviews","Resource locks on each group"],2,"Access reviews run recurring reviews of group memberships, app assignments or role assignments and can auto-remove denied access. Policy compliance does not review who has access.","1: Governance",["Azure Policy compliance reports show resource configuration compliance, not who has access to resource groups.","Secure score measures overall security posture and does not review guest access assignments.",null,"Resource locks prevent changes to resources; they do not review or remove user access."],2],["az22",0,1,"Microsoft Entra ID Protection reports a user as high risk after leaked credentials were found. Which control forces a secure password change at next sign-in?",["A user risk Conditional Access policy requiring password change","A sign-in frequency session control set to one hour","A named location that marks the office network as trusted","A Key Vault access policy that removes the user's secrets"],0,"User risk reflects the likelihood an identity is compromised, and a risk-based policy can require a secure password change with MFA. Sign-in frequency only changes how often users reauthenticate.","1: Identity protection",[null,"Sign-in frequency only changes how often users reauthenticate; it does not force a secure password reset after a leak.","A named location marks a network as trusted and does nothing to remediate a high-risk user.","A Key Vault access policy controls vault permissions, not the user's account password."],2],["az23",0,2,"A developer needs to give a partner time-limited read access to one blob container. The storage account has key access disabled. Which SAS type can still be used?",["Account SAS","Service SAS","Stored access policy SAS","User delegation SAS"],3,"A user delegation SAS is signed with a key obtained through Entra credentials, so it works when Shared Key is disabled. Account and service SAS are signed with the account key.","2: Storage",["An account SAS is signed with the account key, which is disabled here.","A service SAS is also signed with the account key and is unavailable when Shared Key is disabled.","A stored access policy still governs a service or account SAS signed with the account key, so it cannot be used either.",null],3],["az24",0,2,"A service SAS was issued with a one-year expiry and must be revoked today without affecting other SAS tokens signed with the same key. What should have been done when it was issued?",["Sign it with the secondary account key","Link it to a stored access policy","Set the HTTPS-only protocol flag on it","Include a narrow allowed IP address range"],1,"A SAS tied to a stored access policy can be revoked by changing or deleting that policy. Rotating the signing key would invalidate every SAS signed with it.","2: Storage",["Signing with the secondary key does not allow revoking one token without invalidating every SAS signed with that key.",null,"The HTTPS-only flag enforces transport security but does not make a SAS revocable.","A narrow IP range limits where the SAS works but cannot revoke it before its expiry."],3],["az25",0,2,"Compliance requires that financial records in Blob Storage cannot be modified or deleted for seven years, even by account owners. What should be configured?",["Soft delete for blobs with a 7-year retention","A locked time-based immutability (WORM) policy","A CanNotDelete resource lock on the account","Customer-managed keys with a rotation policy"],1,"A locked time-based retention policy makes blobs write-once, read-many for the retention period. A resource lock blocks deleting the account, not changing blob data.","2: Storage",["Soft delete retains deleted blobs for recovery but does not stop modification or deletion, which owners can still perform.",null,"A CanNotDelete lock blocks deleting the storage account, not modifying or deleting blob data within it.","Customer-managed keys control encryption, not blob immutability."],2],["az26",0,2,"A regulator requires that the company control and be able to revoke the keys that encrypt a storage account. What should be enabled?",["Infrastructure encryption with Microsoft-managed keys","HTTPS-only secure transfer","A user delegation SAS","Customer-managed keys stored in Key Vault"],3,"Customer-managed keys let the organization own, rotate and revoke the key in Key Vault or Managed HSM. Infrastructure encryption adds a second layer but with Microsoft-managed keys.","2: Storage",["Infrastructure encryption adds a second encryption layer, but with Microsoft-managed keys the organization still cannot revoke them.","HTTPS-only protects data in transit and has nothing to do with encryption key ownership.","A user delegation SAS is an access token, not an encryption key you can control or revoke.",null],2],["az27",0,2,"Uploaded files in a storage account should be checked for malware as they arrive, and the result written as a blob index tag. Which feature fits?",["Defender for Storage malware scanning","An Azure Policy DeployIfNotExists assignment","Blob soft delete with versioning turned on","Network Watcher packet capture on the subnet"],0,"Defender for Storage on-upload malware scanning scans new blobs and can tag results and raise alerts. The other options do not inspect file content.","2: Storage",[null,"A DeployIfNotExists policy remediates configuration; it does not inspect file content for malware.","Soft delete with versioning retains blob versions but does not scan uploads for malware.","Network Watcher packet capture records network traffic, not blob file content."],2],["az28",0,2,"A storage account firewall allows only selected VNets. Azure Backup and Azure Monitor now fail to reach it. What is the simplest fix?",["Enable public network access from all networks instead","Rotate both account keys and update the services","Allow trusted Microsoft services to access the account","Disable secure transfer so the services can use HTTP"],2,"The trusted Microsoft services exception lets listed Azure services reach the account despite network rules. Opening all networks removes the protection entirely.","2: Storage",["Opening the account to all networks removes the firewall protection entirely.","Rotating the account keys does not grant those services a network path through the firewall.",null,"Disabling secure transfer allows HTTP and weakens security without fixing the network reachability."],2],["az29",0,2,"An Azure SQL logical server must allow only Microsoft Entra identities to sign in, and SQL authentication must be refused. What should be enabled?",["Transparent data encryption","Dynamic data masking","Microsoft Entra-only authentication","Advanced Threat Protection email alerts"],2,"Entra-only authentication disables SQL logins for the server. TDE encrypts data at rest and does not affect sign-in.","2: Databases",["TDE encrypts data at rest and does not change which authentication methods are allowed.","Dynamic data masking obscures values in query results, not sign-in methods.",null,"Advanced Threat Protection email alerts notify on threats; they do not disable SQL authentication."],1],["az30",0,2,"A payroll app stores national ID numbers in Azure SQL. DBAs must not be able to see plaintext values, even with sysadmin rights. What should be used?",["Always Encrypted","Dynamic data masking","Transparent data encryption","Row-level security"],0,"Always Encrypted encrypts columns on the client; keys never reach the database engine, so DBAs see only ciphertext. Dynamic data masking can be bypassed by privileged users and TDE decrypts data for any authorized query.","2: Databases",[null,"Dynamic data masking hides values in result sets, but users with UNMASK (such as DBAs) can still see plaintext.","TDE encrypts at rest but decrypts data for any authorized query, so sysadmins would see plaintext.","Row-level security filters which rows a user can see; it does not hide column values from privileged users."],3],["az31",0,2,"Support staff should see only the last four digits of customer phone numbers in query results, while the app sees full values. No application changes are allowed. What fits?",["Always Encrypted","TDE with customer-managed keys","Server-level firewall rules","Dynamic data masking"],3,"Dynamic data masking hides data in result sets for users without UNMASK permission, with no app changes. Always Encrypted requires driver changes and hides the whole value.","2: Databases",["Always Encrypted hides the entire value and requires driver and application changes, which are not allowed here, and would block the app too.","TDE with customer-managed keys encrypts at rest and does not mask specific fields in results.","Server-level firewall rules control network access, not what data users see in results.",null],2],["az32",0,2,"The security team wants to be alerted to SQL injection attempts and anomalous logins against Azure SQL databases. What should be enabled?",["SQL auditing to a storage account only","Microsoft Defender for SQL","Always Encrypted","Geo-replication"],1,"Defender for SQL provides vulnerability assessment and threat detection, including SQL injection and unusual access alerts. Auditing records events but does not analyze them for threats.","2: Databases",["Auditing to storage records events but does not analyze them for injection or anomalous logins.",null,"Always Encrypted protects column confidentiality; it does not detect attacks.","Geo-replication provides availability and disaster recovery, not threat detection."],2],["az33",0,2,"An NSG has rule 200 denying TCP 3389 from Internet and rule 100 allowing TCP 3389 from 203.0.113.10. What happens to RDP from 203.0.113.10?",["It is denied, because deny rules always take precedence","It is allowed, because rule 100 is processed first","It is denied by the DenyAllInBound default rule","It depends on the route table attached to the subnet"],1,"NSG rules are processed in priority order from lowest number, and the first match decides. Deny rules do not automatically override allows.","2: Networking",["Deny rules do not automatically take precedence; NSGs evaluate by priority order.",null,"Rule 100 matches the traffic first at a lower priority number, so the DenyAllInBound default never applies.","The route table affects the traffic path, not the NSG allow-or-deny decision."],2],["az34",0,2,"Web and database VMs share one subnet. The team wants NSG rules that say 'web servers may reach database servers on 1433' without listing IP addresses. What should be used?",["Service tags","User-defined routes","Private DNS zone records","Application security groups"],3,"Application security groups group VM NICs by role so NSG rules can reference them instead of IPs. Service tags represent Azure service address ranges, not your VMs.","2: Networking",["Service tags represent Microsoft's service address ranges, not your own virtual machines.","User-defined routes control traffic paths; they do not group VMs for NSG rules.","Private DNS zone records resolve names and do not group VMs for rule references.",null],2],["az35",0,2,"An NSG outbound rule should allow VMs to reach Azure Storage in their region only, without tracking Microsoft's IP ranges. What should the destination be?",["The regional Storage service tag","An application security group for storage","The VirtualNetwork service tag","A static list of Azure public IP prefixes"],0,"Regional service tags such as Storage.WestEurope are maintained by Microsoft and update automatically. The VirtualNetwork tag covers your address spaces, not Azure services.","2: Networking",[null,"An application security group groups your VM NICs, not Azure service endpoints like Storage.","The VirtualNetwork service tag covers your own VNet address spaces, not the Azure Storage service.","A static list of prefixes is brittle and requires manual updates as Microsoft changes its ranges."],2],["az36",0,2,"The central network team must block inbound SSH from the internet across 200 VNets, and application teams must not be able to override it with their own NSGs. What should be used?",["An NSG on every subnet that app teams manage themselves","Service endpoints enabled on every subnet in each VNet","Azure Virtual Network Manager security admin rules","Azure DDoS Network Protection on each virtual network"],2,"Security admin rules in Virtual Network Manager are evaluated before NSGs and can deny traffic that NSGs cannot re-allow. NSGs owned by app teams could be changed by those teams.","2: Networking",["An NSG managed by app teams could be changed or overridden by those teams, defeating the central mandate.","Service endpoints extend VNet identity to PaaS services; they do not block inbound SSH.",null,"DDoS Network Protection mitigates volumetric attacks and does not block a specific port."],3],["az37",0,2,"An Azure SQL database must be reachable only through a private IP from the VNet and from on-premises over ExpressRoute, with public access disabled. What should be implemented?",["A service endpoint for Microsoft.Sql on the subnet","A server firewall rule for the VNet address range","A private endpoint with a private DNS zone","An application security group for the database tier"],2,"A private endpoint gives the database a private IP reachable from the VNet, peered VNets and on-premises. Service endpoints only work from the enabled subnet and keep the public endpoint.","2: Networking",["A service endpoint only works from the enabled subnet and keeps the public endpoint reachable.","A server firewall rule for the VNet range still uses the public endpoint and does not provide a private path over ExpressRoute.",null,"An application security group groups VM NICs; it does not give the PaaS database a private IP."],3],["az38",0,2,"After creating a private endpoint for a storage account, VMs still resolve the account's name to its public IP. What is most likely missing?",["A privatelink private DNS zone linked to the VNet","An NSG rule allowing outbound port 443 to Storage","Customer-managed keys configured on the account","A user-defined route sending 0.0.0.0/0 to the internet"],0,"Name resolution must return the private IP, which is done by the privatelink private DNS zone linked to the VNet (or equivalent custom DNS). NSG rules do not change DNS answers.","2: Networking",[null,"An NSG rule for port 443 permits traffic but does not change DNS resolution to return the private IP.","Customer-managed keys concern encryption and are unrelated to name resolution.","A user-defined route to the internet would push traffic away from the private endpoint, not fix resolution."],3],["az39",0,2,"Outbound HTTPS traffic from workloads must be decrypted and checked against intrusion signatures. Which service meets this?",["Azure Firewall Standard","NSG flow logs","Application Gateway Basic","Azure Firewall Premium"],3,"Azure Firewall Premium adds TLS inspection and signature-based IDPS. Standard offers threat intelligence filtering but does not decrypt TLS.","2: Networking",["Azure Firewall Standard offers threat intelligence filtering but does not decrypt TLS or provide full IDPS.","NSG flow logs record flow metadata; they cannot inspect decrypted payloads.","Application Gateway Basic is a layer-7 load balancer and does not provide TLS inspection with IDPS.",null],1],["az40",0,2,"All internet-bound traffic from a spoke subnet must pass through an Azure Firewall in the hub. What must be configured on the spoke subnet?",["An NSG on the spoke subnet that denies all outbound internet traffic","A route table with 0.0.0.0/0 pointing to the firewall's private IP","A service endpoint for Microsoft.Web on the spoke subnet","A private DNS zone for the firewall linked to the spoke VNet"],1,"A user-defined route for 0.0.0.0/0 with the firewall's private IP as the virtual appliance next hop forces traffic through it. An NSG deny would block the traffic instead of redirecting it.","2: Networking",["An NSG outbound deny would block the traffic rather than redirect it through the firewall.",null,"A service endpoint for Microsoft.Web extends VNet identity to a PaaS service; it does not route internet-bound traffic.","A private DNS zone for the firewall resolves names and does not force the traffic path."],3],["az41",0,2,"A public web app behind Application Gateway suffers SQL injection and cross-site scripting attempts. What should be enabled on the gateway?",["DDoS Network Protection on the gateway's VNet","Web Application Firewall with a managed rule set","NSG flow logs on the gateway subnet","A private endpoint for the backend app"],1,"WAF on Application Gateway inspects HTTP requests using managed OWASP-based rules to block injection and XSS. DDoS Protection handles volumetric attacks, not application-layer payloads.","2: Networking",["DDoS Network Protection mitigates volumetric network floods, not application-layer injection or XSS payloads.",null,"NSG flow logs record traffic metadata and cannot block malicious HTTP requests.","A private endpoint for the backend restricts network access but does not inspect requests for injection attacks."],2],["az42",0,2,"A company wants to publish a global web app with edge caching and layer 7 protection close to users worldwide. Which combination fits?",["Application Gateway with a public IP in one region","Azure Firewall Standard in each spoke","VPN Gateway with forced tunneling","Azure Front Door with a WAF policy"],3,"Front Door is a global edge service and its WAF policy filters traffic at the edge. Application Gateway is regional.","2: Networking",["Application Gateway is a regional service, so a single-region deployment has no global edge caching close to users worldwide.","Azure Firewall Standard per spoke is a network firewall, not a global edge web delivery, caching or WAF service.","A VPN Gateway with forced tunneling provides private connectivity, not public global web delivery.",null],2],["az43",0,2,"Remote workers need access to specific private apps in Azure without a full network-level VPN, based on identity and Conditional Access. What fits?",["Microsoft Entra Private Access","Point-to-site VPN with certificate auth","A site-to-site VPN","Azure Bastion"],0,"Entra Private Access is a zero trust network access service that publishes private apps per identity with Conditional Access. A point-to-site VPN gives network-level access to the VNet.","2: Networking",[null,"A point-to-site VPN grants network-level access to the VNet rather than per-app, identity-based access.","A site-to-site VPN connects entire networks and offers no per-app Conditional Access.","Azure Bastion brokers RDP and SSH to VMs; it is not a general private-app access service."],2],["az44",0,2,"A VM cannot reach a database on port 1433. The engineer wants to know which NSG rule is dropping the packet. Which tool answers this fastest?",["Azure Advisor","Defender for Cloud secure score","Network Watcher IP flow verify","Azure Monitor autoscale"],2,"IP flow verify tests a specific 5-tuple against the effective NSG rules and names the rule that allows or denies it. Advisor gives general recommendations.","2: Networking",["Azure Advisor gives general best-practice recommendations, not per-packet NSG rule evaluation.","Secure score measures posture and does not test a specific 5-tuple against NSG rules.",null,"Autoscale scales compute resources and is unrelated to diagnosing NSG rules."],2],["az45",0,2,"The security team wants a record of accepted and denied traffic across a VNet, with visual analysis of top talkers. What should be enabled?",["Boot diagnostics on every virtual machine","Connection troubleshoot on each VM","VNet flow logs with traffic analytics","Azure Monitor metrics alerts on each NIC"],2,"VNet flow logs record allowed and denied flows to storage and traffic analytics processes them in Log Analytics. Connection troubleshoot tests one connection at a time.","2: Networking",["Boot diagnostics capture a VM's boot output, not network flows.","Connection troubleshoot tests one connection at a time and offers no top-talker analysis.",null,"Metric alerts on NICs fire on thresholds but do not record accepted and denied flows."],2],["az46",0,2,"A company has 30 branch offices and several Azure regions and wants centrally managed connectivity with Azure Firewall inspection in each hub. What fits best?",["Virtual WAN with secured virtual hubs","One hub VNet peered directly to every branch office","A point-to-site VPN client for each branch user","Service endpoints on every subnet in every region"],0,"Virtual WAN provides managed hubs for branch VPN, ExpressRoute and user VPN, and a secured hub adds Azure Firewall managed through Firewall Manager. Branches cannot be peered as VNets.","2: Networking",[null,"Branch offices are physical sites, not VNets, so they cannot be peered to a hub VNet.","A point-to-site client VPN per branch is per-user and does not provide managed site connectivity with per-hub firewalls.","Service endpoints extend a VNet to PaaS services and do not provide branch connectivity."],2],["az47",0,2,"Public IP addresses in front of critical workloads need adaptive tuning, attack telemetry and cost protection against volumetric attacks. What should be enabled?",["Azure Firewall Basic SKU","Front Door WAF custom rules","NSG default inbound deny rules","Azure DDoS Protection"],3,"Azure DDoS Protection adds adaptive tuning, attack metrics and alerts, and cost protection beyond the default infrastructure protection. WAF addresses application-layer attacks.","2: Networking",["Azure Firewall Basic is a firewall SKU, not a volumetric DDoS mitigation with adaptive tuning and cost protection.","Front Door WAF custom rules filter application-layer traffic, not volumetric network attacks.","NSG default deny rules are static and provide no adaptive tuning or attack telemetry.",null],2],["az48",0,3,"Management ports on VMs must be closed by default and opened only for a requested source IP for a few hours. What should be enabled?",["Azure Bastion Developer","Just-in-time VM access","An NSG allowing 3389 from any","Encryption at host"],1,"JIT, part of Defender for Servers Plan 2, keeps ports closed and adds temporary allow rules on approved requests. Bastion removes the need for public ports but does not open them on request.","3: Servers and VMs",["Azure Bastion Developer removes the need for public management ports but does not open them per request for a specific source IP.",null,"An NSG allowing 3389 from any leaves the port open to everyone, the opposite of closed-by-default.","Encryption at host encrypts disks and is unrelated to controlling port access."],1],["az49",0,3,"Admins need RDP and SSH to VMs from the Azure portal without assigning public IPs to the VMs. What should be deployed?",["A NAT gateway","Azure Bastion","Application Gateway","A public load balancer"],1,"Bastion brokers RDP/SSH over TLS from the portal to the VM's private IP. A NAT gateway only handles outbound traffic.","3: Servers and VMs",["A NAT gateway handles outbound traffic only and cannot broker inbound RDP or SSH.",null,"Application Gateway is a layer-7 web load balancer, not an RDP/SSH broker.","A public load balancer distributes traffic and would still require public IPs, not private brokered access."],1],["az50",0,3,"A new Windows VM must be protected against bootkits and rootkits with measured boot and a virtual TPM. Which option should be selected at creation?",["Azure Disk Encryption with BitLocker keys","Encryption at host with platform-managed keys","A dedicated host isolated for the company","Trusted launch (secure boot and vTPM)"],3,"Trusted launch provides secure boot, vTPM and boot integrity monitoring. Disk encryption protects data at rest, not the boot chain.","3: Servers and VMs",["Azure Disk Encryption protects data at rest, not the boot chain against bootkits and rootkits.","Encryption at host encrypts disks and caches but provides no secure boot or vTPM.","A dedicated host isolates the physical hardware but does not provide measured boot or a vTPM.",null],2],["az51",0,3,"VM temporary disks and disk caches must be encrypted, and no agent may run inside the guest OS. What should be enabled?",["Encryption at host","Azure Disk Encryption","Always Encrypted","Storage Service Encryption only"],0,"Encryption at host encrypts temp disks and caches at the host level without an in-guest agent. Azure Disk Encryption uses BitLocker or DM-Crypt inside the guest.","3: Servers and VMs",[null,"Azure Disk Encryption uses an in-guest agent (BitLocker or DM-Crypt), which the requirement explicitly forbids.","Always Encrypted is a SQL column-protection feature, not VM disk encryption.","Storage Service Encryption covers the managed disk storage, not temp disks and host caches, and is not the named agentless feature."],3],["az52",0,3,"On-premises and AWS servers should appear in Defender for Cloud and receive Defender for Servers protection alongside Azure VMs. What should be deployed on those servers?",["An Azure Bastion host in a peered VNet","A site-to-site VPN Gateway to each location","The Azure Connected Machine agent (Azure Arc)","The legacy Log Analytics (MMA) agent"],2,"Azure Arc projects servers outside Azure as Azure resources so Defender for Servers and Azure Policy can apply. The legacy Log Analytics agent is retired. AWS servers can also be onboarded through the AWS connector, which uses Arc.","3: Servers and VMs",["A Bastion host brokers RDP and SSH; it does not onboard servers into Defender for Cloud.","A site-to-site VPN provides network connectivity, not projection and management of servers as Azure resources.",null,"The legacy Log Analytics (MMA) agent is retired and does not project servers as Arc-enabled resources."],2],["az53",0,3,"Which Defender for Servers plan includes just-in-time VM access and file integrity monitoring?",["Plan 1","Foundational CSPM","Plan 2","Defender for Storage"],2,"Plan 2 includes JIT, file integrity monitoring, agentless scanning and more. Plan 1 focuses on Defender for Endpoint integration.","3: Servers and VMs",["Plan 1 focuses on Defender for Endpoint integration and does not include JIT or file integrity monitoring.","Foundational CSPM is free posture management and does not include workload protection features like JIT or FIM.",null,"Defender for Storage protects storage accounts, not virtual machines."],1],["az54",0,3,"The team wants to find missing OS updates on Azure and Arc-enabled servers and schedule patching windows. Which service fits?",["Azure Update Manager","Azure Advisor recommendations","Network Watcher connection monitor","Microsoft Purview Audit"],0,"Azure Update Manager assesses and installs updates on Azure and Arc-enabled machines with maintenance schedules. Advisor does not deploy patches.","3: Servers and VMs",[null,"Azure Advisor recommends best practices but does not assess or install OS updates.","Network Watcher connection monitor tests network connectivity, not patching.","Microsoft Purview Audit logs activity and does not manage updates."],2],["az55",0,3,"Developers use kubectl with a shared admin kubeconfig for an AKS cluster. Security wants per-user sign-in with Entra ID and access governed by Azure roles. What should be enabled?",["Kubernetes network policies on each namespace","A private cluster with no public API endpoint","Defender for Containers runtime protection","Entra ID integration with Azure RBAC for Kubernetes"],3,"Entra integration authenticates users with Entra ID, and Azure RBAC for Kubernetes authorizes them through Azure role assignments; local accounts can then be disabled. Network policies control pod traffic, not user access.","3: App platforms",["Kubernetes network policies control pod-to-pod traffic, not user authentication to the cluster.","A private cluster hides the API endpoint but does not provide per-user Entra sign-in.","Defender for Containers provides threat protection, not user authentication or authorization.",null],2],["az56",0,3,"The AKS API server must not be reachable from the internet at all. What should be configured?",["Authorized IP ranges only","A private cluster","A Kubernetes network policy","An Azure Policy add-on"],1,"A private cluster gives the API server a private IP only reachable from the VNet. Authorized IP ranges still expose a public endpoint, just filtered.","3: App platforms",["Authorized IP ranges still expose a filtered public API endpoint rather than making it fully private.",null,"A Kubernetes network policy controls pod traffic, not exposure of the API server.","An Azure Policy add-on enforces cluster governance and does not make the API server private."],1],["az57",0,3,"Pods in the 'frontend' namespace must be allowed to reach 'backend' pods, and all other pod-to-pod traffic to backend must be blocked. What should be used?",["An NSG on the node pool subnet","Kubernetes network policies","Azure Firewall DNAT rules for the pods","Azure Bastion connected to the cluster"],1,"Network policies (Azure, Calico or Cilium) filter traffic between pods using labels and namespaces. NSGs work on node or subnet IPs and cannot see pod labels.","3: App platforms",["An NSG on the node subnet works on node and subnet IPs and cannot see pod labels or namespaces.",null,"Azure Firewall DNAT rules handle inbound NAT, not pod-to-pod filtering by label.","Azure Bastion is an RDP/SSH broker and is unrelated to pod traffic filtering."],2],["az58",0,3,"A security team wants vulnerability assessment of container images, runtime threat detection on AKS nodes, and Kubernetes posture recommendations. Which plan provides this?",["Defender for App Service","Defender for Key Vault","Defender for Storage","Defender for Containers"],3,"Defender for Containers covers image vulnerability assessment, run-time protection and Kubernetes hardening for AKS and other clusters. The other plans cover different resource types.","3: App platforms",["Defender for App Service protects web apps, not containers or AKS clusters.","Defender for Key Vault protects vault access, not container images.","Defender for Storage protects storage accounts, not clusters.",null],1],["az59",0,3,"AKS pulls images from Azure Container Registry using the registry's admin user password stored as a Kubernetes secret. What is the recommended replacement?",["Give the kubelet identity AcrPull and disable the admin user","Keep the admin user but rotate its password every month","Store the admin password in a ConfigMap instead of a secret","Make the registry public so no credentials are needed"],0,"Attaching ACR with the kubelet managed identity and AcrPull gives least-privilege, secretless pulls. Rotating a shared admin password keeps a shared credential with push rights.","3: App platforms",[null,"Keeping the admin user and rotating its password monthly still relies on a shared credential that includes push rights, a higher risk than least-privilege pull.","A ConfigMap is not meant for secrets and still exposes the shared admin credential.","Making the registry public exposes all images to anyone, a serious data exposure."],3],["az60",0,3,"An App Service app stores a database connection string in plain text in app settings. The team wants it kept in Key Vault without code changes. What should they use?",["A deployment slot setting marked sticky to production","An Azure Policy Modify effect that encrypts app settings","A Key Vault reference using the app's managed identity","Easy Auth with Microsoft Entra ID for the web app"],2,"A Key Vault reference (@Microsoft.KeyVault(...)) resolves the secret at runtime using the app's managed identity, with no code change. Slot settings only control swap behavior.","3: App platforms",["A sticky slot setting controls swap behavior between deployment slots; it does not move the secret into Key Vault.","An Azure Policy Modify effect changes properties such as tags; it does not fetch secrets from Key Vault.",null,"Easy Auth authenticates end users to the app and does not store the connection string in Key Vault."],2],["az61",0,3,"An internal App Service app should accept traffic only from the company's Application Gateway subnet. What should be configured?",["HTTPS only enabled on the web app","A minimum inbound TLS version of 1.2","Access restrictions allowing that subnet","A custom domain with a managed certificate"],2,"Access restrictions filter inbound requests by IP, service tag or VNet subnet. HTTPS only and TLS version protect traffic in transit but do not restrict sources.","3: App platforms",["HTTPS only encrypts traffic in transit and does not limit which sources can reach the app.","A minimum TLS version sets the protocol floor and does not filter inbound sources.",null,"A custom domain with a managed certificate handles naming and TLS, not source restriction."],2],["az62",0,3,"A company wants users to sign in to an App Service web app with Microsoft Entra ID without writing authentication code. What should be enabled?",["App Service built-in authentication (Easy Auth)","A private endpoint for the web app's inbound traffic","Access restrictions that allow only the corporate IPs","A system-assigned managed identity on the web app"],0,"Built-in authentication handles sign-in with Entra ID and other providers before requests reach the code. A managed identity lets the app call other services, not users sign in.","3: App platforms",[null,"A private endpoint restricts the inbound network path; it does not provide user sign-in.","Access restrictions filter source IPs but do not authenticate users.","A system-assigned managed identity lets the app call other services, not sign users in."],1],["az63",0,3,"An HTTP-triggered Azure Function uses a function key that has been shared widely. Which change best reduces risk while keeping callers working after an update?",["Change the authorization level to anonymous","Move the function key into the URL query string","Enable remote debugging to trace the callers","Require Entra ID authentication and use tokens"],3,"Using Entra authentication gives identity-based, revocable access instead of a shared key. Anonymous removes protection entirely.","3: App platforms",["Setting the authorization level to anonymous removes protection entirely.","Putting the key in the URL query string keeps a shared key and makes it more likely to be logged or leaked.","Remote debugging is a diagnostic feature and does nothing to secure access.",null],3],["az64",0,3,"A Logic App must call an Azure Resource Manager API. The team wants no stored credentials in the workflow. What should they use?",["A client secret in a parameter","A managed identity on the Logic App","A storage account key","A SAS token in the trigger URL"],1,"Logic Apps can authenticate outbound calls with a managed identity that holds the needed Azure role. A client secret is still a stored credential.","3: App platforms",["A client secret in a parameter is still a stored credential that must be managed and rotated.",null,"A storage account key is a stored credential and is the wrong mechanism for calling the ARM API.","A SAS token in the trigger URL is a stored token, not identity-based authentication for ARM."],1],["az65",0,3,"A generative AI chatbot built on Microsoft Foundry is being hit with prompts that try to override its system instructions. The security team wants alerts in Defender for Cloud. What should be enabled?",["Defender for Storage on the training data","Defender for AI services threat protection","Just-in-time VM access for the app servers","Azure DDoS Protection on the public endpoint"],1,"Defender for AI services raises alerts for threats such as jailbreak and prompt injection attempts against AI workloads, using Prompt Shields signals. DDoS Protection handles network floods.","3: Security for AI",["Defender for Storage protects storage accounts, not runtime prompt-injection attempts against an AI app.",null,"Just-in-time VM access controls VM management ports and is unrelated to prompt attacks.","DDoS Protection mitigates network floods, not prompt injection."],2],["az66",0,3,"Several apps call the same Azure OpenAI deployment with API keys, and one app consumes most of the tokens. The team wants central authentication and per-app token limits. What should be deployed?",["A private endpoint for each calling app","A separate Azure OpenAI resource per app","An NSG in front of the OpenAI resource","An AI Gateway in API Management"],3,"An API Management AI gateway can authenticate to the backend with a managed identity and apply token limit policies per consumer. Private endpoints restrict network paths but do not enforce quotas.","3: Security for AI",["A private endpoint per app restricts the network path but does not enforce per-app token quotas.","A separate Azure OpenAI resource per app multiplies resources without central authentication or quota control.","An NSG filters network traffic and cannot meter tokens or centrally authenticate callers.",null],3],["az67",0,3,"Before rolling out Microsoft 365 Copilot, the company wants to find sensitive files that are overshared and could surface in Copilot answers. Which tool fits?",["Microsoft Purview DSPM for AI","Azure Firewall Premium TLS inspection","Defender for Servers file integrity monitoring","Network Watcher traffic analytics"],0,"Purview Data Security Posture Management for AI reports oversharing and sensitive data exposure risks for Copilot and other AI apps. The other tools do not analyze data exposure in Microsoft 365.","3: Security for AI",[null,"Azure Firewall Premium TLS inspection examines network traffic, not oversharing of Microsoft 365 data.","Defender for Servers file integrity monitoring watches file changes on servers, not Copilot data exposure.","Network Watcher traffic analytics analyzes network flows, not data exposure to AI apps."],2],["az68",0,3,"AI agents in the tenant act with their own identities. The security team wants to block agents from signing in when they are flagged as risky. Which feature supports this?",["A resource lock on the agent","An NSG on the agent subnet","Conditional Access for Microsoft Entra Agent ID","Azure Policy Audit effect"],2,"Microsoft Entra Agent ID gives agents identities that Conditional Access policies can target, including risk-based blocking. Resource locks and NSGs do not control identity sign-in.","3: Security for AI",["A resource lock prevents changes to a resource; it does not control identity sign-in.","An NSG on the agent subnet filters network traffic, not identity risk at sign-in.",null,"An Azure Policy Audit effect flags configuration compliance; it does not block a risky agent from signing in."],2],["az69",0,3,"A Foundry model should reject inputs that contain hate speech and should flag outputs that include protected material. What should be configured?",["A WAF custom rule on the Front Door profile","Key Vault purge protection on the model's vault","Content filters and guardrails on the deployment","Transparent data encryption on the chat history"],2,"Foundry deployments use content filters and guardrails (Azure AI Content Safety) to screen prompts and completions. A WAF inspects HTTP patterns, not model content categories.","3: Security for AI",["A WAF custom rule on Front Door inspects HTTP patterns, not model content categories such as hate speech.","Key Vault purge protection protects vault objects and is unrelated to content moderation.",null,"TDE on chat history encrypts stored data; it does not screen inputs or outputs for content."],2],["az70",0,4,"The security team wants to see chains such as 'internet-exposed VM with a critical vulnerability and a managed identity that can read sensitive storage'. Which Defender for Cloud feature shows this?",["Attack path analysis in Defender CSPM","The regulatory compliance dashboard view","Workflow automation with a Logic App","Secure score history in the overview blade"],0,"Attack path analysis, part of the paid Defender CSPM plan, maps exploitable paths to high-value assets. The compliance dashboard only maps controls to standards.","4: Defender for Cloud",[null,"The regulatory compliance dashboard maps controls to standards; it does not chart exploitable attack chains.","Workflow automation runs Logic Apps in response to events; it does not visualize attack paths.","Secure score history trends the score over time and does not map exploit chains to assets."],3],["az71",0,4,"Which capability is included in free foundational CSPM?",["Attack path analysis","Agentless vulnerability scanning","Cloud security explorer queries","Secure score and security recommendations"],3,"Foundational CSPM includes secure score, recommendations, asset inventory and the Microsoft cloud security benchmark. The others require Defender CSPM.","4: Defender for Cloud",["Attack path analysis is a paid Defender CSPM feature, not part of free foundational CSPM.","Agentless vulnerability scanning requires Defender CSPM or workload plans, not free CSPM.","Cloud security explorer is part of the paid Defender CSPM plan.",null],1],["az72",0,4,"An engineer fixes a recommendation on 8 of 10 storage accounts. How does secure score change for that control?",["It gains the full control score immediately","It gains points in proportion to the healthy resources","It does not change until the next monthly recalculation","It drops, because partial remediation is penalized"],1,"Secure score awards control points in proportion to healthy resources, and the full points only when every resource is healthy. Scores refresh within hours, not monthly.","4: Defender for Cloud",["The full control score is awarded only when every resource is healthy, not after fixing 8 of 10.",null,"Secure score refreshes within hours, not on a monthly recalculation.","Partial remediation raises the score proportionally; it is not penalized with a drop."],3],["az73",0,4,"An auditor wants to see how the Azure environment maps to PCI DSS controls. Where should the engineer add this?",["Import a PCI template from the Sentinel workbooks gallery","Add the standard in regulatory compliance","Filter the Azure Advisor recommendations by PCI tag","Run an Entra ID access review scoped to cardholder apps"],1,"The regulatory compliance dashboard assesses resources against the Microsoft cloud security benchmark and extra standards such as PCI DSS that you add. Workbooks visualize data but do not assess compliance.","4: Defender for Cloud",["Workbooks visualize data and do not assess resources against a compliance standard like PCI DSS.",null,"Azure Advisor gives recommendations and has no PCI compliance mapping to filter by.","An Entra access review evaluates who has access; it does not map resources to PCI controls."],2],["az74",0,4,"Recommendations keep being ignored. The CISO wants each one assigned to a resource owner with a due date and email reminders. What should be configured?",["A Sentinel watchlist","An Azure Policy exemption","A resource lock","Governance rules in Defender for Cloud"],3,"Governance rules assign owners and due dates to recommendations automatically and send reminders. An exemption would hide the recommendation instead of fixing it.","4: Defender for Cloud",["A Sentinel watchlist is a reference list for KQL, not a way to assign recommendation owners or reminders.","An Azure Policy exemption hides the recommendation rather than driving a fix.","A resource lock prevents resource changes and does not assign owners or due dates.",null],2],["az75",0,4,"The company also runs workloads in AWS and wants their posture in the same Defender for Cloud secure score. What should be created?",["An AWS connector in Defender for Cloud","A site-to-site VPN between Azure and AWS","A Sentinel watchlist that lists the AWS account IDs","A management group named AWS with the accounts in it"],0,"Multicloud connectors onboard AWS accounts and GCP projects for posture management and workload protection. A VPN only provides network connectivity.","4: Defender for Cloud",[null,"A site-to-site VPN provides network connectivity, not posture onboarding into secure score.","A Sentinel watchlist of account IDs is a reference list and does not bring AWS posture into Defender for Cloud.","A management group organizes Azure subscriptions, not AWS accounts."],2],["az76",0,4,"Every high-severity Defender for Cloud alert should automatically trigger a Logic App that opens a ticket. What should be configured?",["A Sentinel hunting query","An Azure Policy Audit effect","Workflow automation in Defender for Cloud","A Key Vault rotation policy"],2,"Workflow automation runs a Logic App when alerts, recommendations or compliance changes match your conditions. Hunting queries are run manually.","4: Defender for Cloud",["A Sentinel hunting query is run manually and does not auto-trigger on Defender for Cloud alerts.","An Azure Policy Audit effect flags configuration compliance and does not run a Logic App on alerts.",null,"A Key Vault rotation policy rotates keys and is unrelated to alert automation."],1],["az77",0,4,"A Linux appliance sends CEF-formatted logs. How should they be collected into Microsoft Sentinel today?",["The legacy Log Analytics agent installed directly on the appliance","A daily script that uploads the log files to a storage account","A Linux forwarder running the Azure Monitor Agent with a CEF via AMA rule","The appliance configured to send syslog to the Sentinel portal URL"],2,"The supported method uses the Azure Monitor Agent on a forwarder and a data collection rule through the CEF via AMA connector. The legacy Log Analytics agent is retired.","4: Sentinel",["The legacy Log Analytics agent installed on the appliance is retired and is not the supported CEF ingestion path.","A daily script uploading files to storage is not a supported real-time CEF ingestion method for Sentinel.",null,"Sentinel has no direct portal syslog endpoint; CEF requires a forwarder running the Azure Monitor Agent."],2],["az78",0,4,"An analyst wants a Sentinel rule that detects role assignment creation within about a minute of it happening. Which rule type is designed for this?",["Near-real-time (NRT) analytics rule","Scheduled rule running every 24 hours","Anomaly rule","Hunting bookmark"],0,"NRT rules run every minute over the latest data for fast detection. A daily scheduled rule would be too slow, and bookmarks are not detections.","4: Sentinel",[null,"A rule scheduled every 24 hours is far too slow for near-real-time detection within about a minute.","An anomaly rule detects statistical deviations and is not designed for ~1-minute event detection.","A hunting bookmark saves query results and is not an automated detection."],1],["az79",0,4,"Sentinel incidents from a noisy test subscription should automatically be closed as benign, and all others assigned to the tier 1 queue. What should be used?",["A playbook for each incident","A workbook","A watchlist","Automation rules"],3,"Automation rules handle simple triage such as changing status, assigning owners and adding tags without a Logic App. Playbooks are for external or multi-step actions.","4: Sentinel",["A playbook is a Logic App for external or multi-step actions, which is overkill for simple status changes and assignment.","A workbook visualizes data and does not triage incidents.","A watchlist is a reference list and does not close or assign incidents.",null],1],["az80",0,4,"When a Sentinel incident involves a compromised user, the SOC wants to disable the account in Entra ID and post in a Teams channel automatically. What should be built?",["An analytics rule with entity mapping enabled","A playbook triggered by an automation rule","A saved hunting query with a livestream","A data collection rule with a transformation"],1,"Playbooks carry out external actions such as disabling users or posting messages, and an automation rule can trigger them on incident creation. Entity mapping only identifies entities.","4: Sentinel",["An analytics rule with entity mapping identifies entities but does not disable users or post messages.",null,"A hunting query with a livestream monitors for hits; it does not take external actions.","A data collection rule with a transformation shapes ingested data and cannot disable users or post to Teams."],2],["az81",0,4,"Analytics rule results should show the affected user and host so incidents can be correlated and investigated in the graph. What should be configured in the rule?",["Alert grouping disabled","Entity mapping","A shorter lookback","Suppression"],1,"Entity mapping tells Sentinel which query columns are accounts, hosts, IPs and so on, enabling correlation and investigation. Suppression stops the rule from running after a match.","4: Sentinel",["Disabling alert grouping changes how alerts bundle into incidents; it does not label entity columns.",null,"A shorter lookback changes the query time window, not entity correlation.","Suppression stops the rule from running after a match and does not map entities."],1],["az82",0,4,"The SOC maintains a list of VIP accounts that analytics rules should treat as high priority. Where should the list live so KQL can reference it?",["A Key Vault secret","An Azure Policy parameter","A resource tag","A Sentinel watchlist"],3,"Watchlists are uploaded lists that KQL can join with the _GetWatchlist() function. Key Vault and policy parameters are not queryable from Sentinel rules.","4: Sentinel",["A Key Vault secret is not queryable from a KQL analytics rule.","An Azure Policy parameter governs policy evaluation and is not referenceable by Sentinel KQL.","A resource tag is metadata on a resource and is not joinable within analytics rules.",null],1],["az83",0,4,"Firewall logs must be kept for two years for investigations, but are rarely queried and are too costly in the Analytics tier. What should the engineer consider?",["A lower-cost long-term tier such as the Sentinel data lake","Deleting the logs after 30 days to stay within budget","Copying each day's logs into a Sentinel watchlist","Disabling the firewall connector until an incident occurs"],0,"Lower-cost tiers keep high-volume, rarely queried data for long periods at reduced cost while still allowing queries. Deleting logs breaks the retention requirement.","4: Sentinel",[null,"Deleting the logs after 30 days breaks the two-year retention requirement.","A watchlist is a small reference list, not a mechanism for bulk long-term log retention.","Disabling the connector loses the logs entirely, defeating the retention requirement."],3],["az84",0,4,"A threat hunter wants to look proactively for signs of an attacker technique that has no alert yet, and save interesting results for later. What should be used?",["Workflow automation in Defender for Cloud","Automation rules that close incidents","Hunting queries and bookmarks","Secure score recommendations"],2,"Hunting queries search data for indicators, and bookmarks save results for investigation or promotion to incidents. Automation rules act on incidents that already exist.","4: Sentinel",["Workflow automation acts on Defender for Cloud alerts and is not for proactive hunting of undetected techniques.","Automation rules act on existing incidents, not on techniques that have raised no alert yet.",null,"Secure score recommendations provide posture guidance, not proactive threat hunting."],1],["az85",0,4,"Which KQL query returns failed sign-ins from the last hour grouped by user?",["SigninLogs | where TimeGenerated > ago(1h) and ResultType == \"0\" | summarize count() by UserPrincipalName","AuditLogs | where TimeGenerated > ago(1h) | summarize count() by OperationName, Identity","SigninLogs | where TimeGenerated > ago(1h) and ResultType != \"0\" | summarize count() by UserPrincipalName","SecurityEvent | where TimeGenerated > ago(1h) and EventID == 4624 | summarize count() by Account"],2,"ResultType 0 means success, so != \"0\" returns failures, and summarize groups by user. The other queries return successful sign-ins or unrelated data.","4: Sentinel",["ResultType == \"0\" returns successful sign-ins, not failed ones.","AuditLogs summarized by OperationName queries directory audit events, not failed sign-ins.",null,"SecurityEvent EventID 4624 is a successful Windows logon, not an Entra failed sign-in."],2],["az86",0,4,"Users report that Microsoft Security Copilot responses are slow and prompts are being throttled during a busy incident. What is the most likely fix?",["Provision more SCUs or allow overage","Assign all analysts the Security Reader role","Enable more Sentinel data connectors","Disable promptbooks for the whole tenant"],0,"Security Copilot capacity is measured in SCUs, and throttling means capacity is exhausted. Adding roles or connectors does not add capacity.","4: Security Copilot",[null,"Assigning the Security Reader role grants read permissions and does not add compute capacity.","Enabling more Sentinel connectors adds data sources, not Copilot capacity.","Disabling promptbooks removes a feature and does not relieve capacity-based throttling."],2],["az87",0,4,"An analyst wants Security Copilot to run the same series of prompts for every phishing investigation. What should they create?",["A watchlist","A workbook","A data collection rule","A promptbook"],3,"Promptbooks are saved sequences of prompts that can be run together with inputs. Workbooks visualize data and do not run Copilot prompts.","4: Security Copilot",["A watchlist is a Sentinel reference list and cannot run Copilot prompts.","A workbook visualizes data and does not sequence and run prompts.","A data collection rule shapes log ingestion and is unrelated to Copilot prompts.",null],1],["az88",0,4,"Security Copilot needs to answer questions using data from a third-party threat intelligence service. What should be added?",["A new SCU region","A plugin for that service","A Sentinel automation rule","A Purview sensitivity label"],1,"Plugins extend Security Copilot with data and skills from Microsoft and third-party sources. SCUs add capacity, not new data sources.","4: Security Copilot",["A new SCU region adds capacity, not new data sources.",null,"A Sentinel automation rule automates incident handling and does not extend Copilot's data.","A Purview sensitivity label classifies data and does not connect a threat intelligence source to Copilot."],1],["az89",0,4,"During an investigation, the team needs to know who deleted a SharePoint file and when. Which tool records this activity?",["Defender for Cloud secure score","Microsoft Purview Audit","Network Watcher flow logs","Azure Update Manager"],1,"Purview Audit (unified audit log) records user and admin activity across Microsoft 365, including file deletions. Flow logs only record network traffic.","4: Operations",["Secure score measures security posture and does not record who deleted a specific file.",null,"Network Watcher flow logs record network traffic, not file activity in Microsoft 365.","Azure Update Manager manages patching and does not record audit activity."],1],["az90",0,4,"A company wants Microsoft Defender XDR incidents, including those from Defender for Cloud, to flow into Sentinel without creating duplicate incidents. What should be used?",["A separate scheduled analytics rule for each Defender alert","A daily CSV export of alerts imported into a custom table","A watchlist of alert IDs joined to the SecurityAlert table","The Defender XDR connector with incident integration"],3,"The Defender XDR connector syncs incidents and alerts bidirectionally, avoiding duplicates. Separate rules per alert would create duplicate incidents.","4: Sentinel",["A separate scheduled analytics rule per Defender alert would create duplicate incidents.","A daily CSV export into a custom table is manual, delayed, and does not sync incidents without duplicates.","A watchlist of alert IDs joined to SecurityAlert is a reference list, not a bidirectional incident sync.",null],3],["x1",0,1,"A custom Azure role was created with assignableScopes set to one subscription. An administrator now tries to assign the role in a second subscription and cannot find it in the role list. What should be changed?",["The role's notActions list, to remove the operations it blocks","The role's dataActions list, to add the missing data permissions","The role's assignableScopes, to include the second subscription","The administrator's PIM activation, to a longer maximum duration"],2,"A custom role can only be assigned at the scopes listed in its assignableScopes, so the second subscription must be added there. Changing notActions or dataActions alters what the role permits, not where it can be assigned.","Domain 1: Custom RBAC roles",["notActions subtracts operations from the role; it has no effect on which scopes the role can be assigned at.","dataActions defines data-plane permissions, not where the role definition is available for assignment.",null,"PIM activation duration controls how long an assignment is active, not whether a role is visible at a scope."],2],["x2",0,1,"A custom role grants Microsoft.Compute/* with notActions containing Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/delete. The same user also holds Contributor on the resource group. Can the user delete VMs in that resource group?",["Yes; notActions only trims that role and does not deny","No; notActions works as a deny across every role the user holds","No; the most restrictive role assignment always takes priority","Yes, but only after the custom role is activated through PIM"],0,"Azure RBAC is additive: notActions subtracts permissions from that one role definition only, so Contributor still grants delete. It is not a deny assignment, which is the tempting but wrong reading.","Domain 1: Custom RBAC roles",[null,"notActions is not a deny assignment; it only removes permissions from its own role, so Contributor still allows delete.","Azure RBAC is additive; the union of all assignments applies, not the most restrictive one.","PIM activation is unrelated; Contributor already grants delete without activating the custom role."],3],["x3",0,1,"Activating the Global Administrator role in Privileged Identity Management must require sign-off from a second person before the role becomes active. Which PIM role setting provides this?",["Require MFA on activation","Set the maximum activation duration to one hour","Send activation notifications to the default recipients","Require approval to activate, with named approvers"],3,"PIM's 'require approval to activate' setting holds the request until a designated approver approves it. MFA on activation proves the requester's identity but involves no second person.","Domain 1: Privileged Identity Management",["MFA on activation verifies the requester but never involves a second person approving the request.","A shorter activation window limits exposure time but lets the role activate without anyone else's sign-off.","Notifications tell people after activation happens; they do not block activation until someone approves.",null],1],["x4",0,1,"A single-tenant service principal used by a CI pipeline should only be able to obtain tokens when calling from the build agents' public IP range. What should be configured?",["A Conditional Access policy for all users and guests, with a named location condition","A Conditional Access policy targeting workload identities with a location condition","An Azure Policy Deny effect on the service principal's role assignments","A PIM eligible assignment for the service principal's Contributor role"],1,"Conditional Access for workload identities can target service principals and block them outside named locations. A policy scoped to users and guests does not evaluate service principal sign-ins at all.","Domain 1: Conditional Access for workload identities",["Policies scoped to users and guests do not apply to service principal token requests, so the pipeline is unaffected.",null,"Azure Policy governs resource properties; it cannot evaluate where a service principal signs in from.","PIM eligibility controls when a role is active, not the network location a service principal authenticates from."],2],["x5",0,1,"Users of a finance app on unmanaged devices must re-authenticate every four hours and must not stay signed in after closing the browser. Which Conditional Access configuration meets this?",["A grant control requiring a compliant device, combined with a block on legacy clients","Session controls: sign-in frequency plus never-persistent browser sessions","A terms of use grant control that users must accept every four hours","An authentication strength of passwordless MFA applied to all cloud apps"],1,"Sign-in frequency forces re-authentication after the set interval and persistent browser session set to 'never persistent' ends the session when the browser closes. Requiring a compliant device would simply block unmanaged devices rather than limit their sessions.","Domain 1: Conditional Access session controls",["Requiring a compliant device blocks unmanaged devices outright instead of limiting their session length and persistence.",null,"Terms of use require acceptance of a document; they do not force re-authentication or end browser sessions.","Authentication strength controls which methods are used, not how often users must sign in again."],2],["x6",0,1,"A new employee must register a passkey on their first day but has no password and no other authentication method yet. Which method lets them bootstrap registration securely?",["An SMS one-time passcode sent to a personal phone number","Security questions that the helpdesk sets up for them","A shared password from their manager, changed later","A Temporary Access Pass issued by an administrator"],3,"A Temporary Access Pass is a time-limited passcode, enabled in the authentication methods policy, designed for onboarding users so they can register strong methods such as passkeys. SMS is a weak, phishable method and still requires an existing registered phone.","Domain 1: Authentication methods policy",["SMS is a weak, phishable method, and the user has no registered phone method to use in the first place.","Security questions are not an Entra sign-in method for bootstrapping a passkey and are easy to guess or research.","A shared password is known to someone else and undermines the goal of a passwordless start.",null],2],["x7",0,1,"A background service with no signed-in user must read every user's calendar through Microsoft Graph. Which permission type does it need, and who grants it?",["Application permission, granted through admin consent","Delegated permission, granted by each user whose calendar is read","Delegated permission, granted once by the developer who registered the app","Application permission, granted by any user through the user consent prompt"],0,"Daemons with no user present use application permissions, and application permissions always require admin consent. Delegated permissions only act on behalf of a signed-in user, which this service does not have.","Domain 1: API permissions and consent",[null,"Delegated permissions need a signed-in user, and this service runs with no user present.","Delegated permissions still act on behalf of a user; the developer's one-time consent cannot cover all calendars.","Application permissions cannot be granted through user consent; they always require an administrator."],2],["x8",0,1,"A Key Vault uses the Azure RBAC permission model. An application only needs to read secret values at runtime. Which built-in role follows least privilege?",["Key Vault Administrator","Key Vault Contributor","Key Vault Secrets User","Key Vault Secrets Officer"],2,"Key Vault Secrets User allows reading secret contents and nothing more. Key Vault Secrets Officer can also create, update and delete secrets, which is more than the app needs.","Domain 1: Key Vault RBAC",["Key Vault Administrator grants full data-plane control over keys, secrets and certificates, far beyond read-only.","Key Vault Contributor manages the vault resource itself and grants no access to secret values.",null,"Key Vault Secrets Officer can create, update and delete secrets, which exceeds a read-only need."],1],["x9",0,1,"The security team wants alerts when a Key Vault is accessed from a Tor exit node or when an unfamiliar application suddenly lists many secrets. What should be enabled?",["Key Vault diagnostic logging to a storage account","Purge protection at the maximum retention period","An Azure Policy Audit assignment covering all vaults","Microsoft Defender for Key Vault"],3,"Defender for Key Vault analyzes vault access and raises threat alerts such as access from suspicious IPs or unusual secret enumeration. Diagnostic logging only records the activity; nothing analyzes it or alerts on it.","Domain 1: Defender for Key Vault",["Diagnostic logs record operations but nothing analyzes them or raises threat alerts on their own.","Purge protection prevents permanent deletion; it does not detect suspicious access patterns.","An Audit policy reports configuration compliance, not runtime access from suspicious sources.",null],1],["x10",0,1,"A Deny policy blocks public IP addresses on NICs across a subscription. One legacy resource group legitimately needs a public IP for 60 days, and the exception must be documented and expire automatically. What should be used?",["Change the policy assignment's effect from Deny to Disabled for the whole subscription","Unassign the policy for 60 days and set a reminder to reassign it","A policy exemption on that resource group, with an expiration date","A resource lock on the group so the policy cannot evaluate it"],2,"A policy exemption removes one scope from evaluation, records a category and justification, and can carry an expiration date. Setting the effect to Disabled would remove protection from every other resource group too.","Domain 1: Azure Policy exemptions",["Disabling the effect turns off the protection for every resource group in the subscription, not just one.","Unassigning leaves the whole subscription unprotected and relies on someone remembering to reassign it.",null,"Resource locks prevent changes or deletion; they do not exclude resources from policy evaluation."],2],["x11",0,1,"Every resource must carry the CostCenter tag of its resource group. New resources should get it automatically, and existing resources missing it must be fixed with a remediation task. Which policy effect fits?",["Append","Modify","Audit","Deny"],1,"Modify can add or change tags on create or update and supports remediation tasks for existing resources through a managed identity. Append can add fields at creation but cannot remediate existing resources.","Domain 1: Azure Policy effects",["Append can add fields at creation but does not support remediation tasks for existing resources.",null,"Audit only reports missing tags; it never adds them to new or existing resources.","Deny blocks non-compliant resources instead of adding the missing tag."],3],["x12",0,1,"A company wants one policy initiative and one RBAC assignment to apply automatically to every current and future subscription. Where should they be assigned?",["A management group that sits above all the subscriptions","Each subscription separately, using a script that runs nightly to catch new ones","The resource group in each subscription that holds shared services","A Microsoft Entra administrative unit containing the subscription owners"],0,"Policy and RBAC assignments at a management group are inherited by every subscription beneath it, including ones added later. A nightly script leaves gaps and drift; administrative units scope Entra roles, not Azure resources.","Domain 1: Management groups",[null,"Per-subscription scripting creates drift and gaps between runs, and new subscriptions are exposed until it runs.","Assigning at one resource group does not cover the rest of the subscription or any other subscription.","Administrative units scope Microsoft Entra directory roles; they cannot hold Azure policy or RBAC assignments."],2],["x13",0,1,"Microsoft Entra ID Protection flags a sign-in as medium risk because it came from an anonymous IP address. Users should be able to self-remediate by completing MFA instead of being blocked. What should be configured?",["A sign-in risk Conditional Access policy that requires MFA","A user risk policy that requires a secure password change","A named location that marks anonymous IPs as trusted","An access review of all users with risky sign-ins"],0,"Sign-in risk policies act on the risk of the current sign-in, and requiring MFA lets the user prove identity and remediate. User risk policies address the likelihood that the account itself is compromised, such as leaked credentials.","Domain 1: ID Protection risk policies",[null,"User risk policies target compromised accounts and require password change, not per-sign-in MFA remediation.","Marking anonymous IPs as trusted would lower detection and could suppress the risk signal entirely.","An access review is periodic and manual; it does not respond to a risky sign-in when it happens."],2],["x14",0,1,"An engineer is about to enforce a Conditional Access policy that requires compliant devices for all users. What should be excluded to avoid locking everyone out if the policy or device management misbehaves?",["All guest users from partner organizations","Two cloud-only emergency access (break-glass) accounts","The Azure portal and Microsoft Graph applications","All users who hold eligible PIM roles"],1,"Emergency access accounts excluded from Conditional Access keep a way back into the tenant if a policy blocks everyone. Excluding the Azure portal and Graph would weaken the policy for every user rather than provide a controlled recovery path.","Domain 1: Conditional Access",["Excluding guests weakens the policy for partners and still leaves every internal admin exposed to a lockout.",null,"Excluding those apps would remove the control for every user on the most sensitive admin interfaces.","Excluding all PIM-eligible users creates a large gap and does not guarantee a working recovery account."],2],["x15",0,2,"After 'Allow storage account key access' is disabled on a storage account, which kind of request stops working?",["Requests that use a user delegation SAS","Requests authorized with Entra ID and Storage Blob Data Reader","Requests that use an account SAS or a service SAS","Requests from managed identities holding data-plane roles"],2,"Account and service SAS tokens are signed with the account key, so they are rejected once Shared Key authorization is disabled. A user delegation SAS is signed with an Entra ID-issued key and keeps working.","Domain 2: Storage authorization",["A user delegation SAS is signed with an Entra-issued key, so it keeps working without Shared Key.","Entra ID authorization with RBAC is the intended path and is unaffected by disabling key access.",null,"Managed identities authenticate with Entra ID tokens, which do not depend on account keys."],3],["x16",0,2,"A migration tool needs one SAS that can list containers in Blob storage and create queues in Queue storage within the same account. Which SAS type supports this?",["User delegation SAS","Service SAS for one blob container","A SAS tied to a stored access policy on a queue","Account SAS"],3,"An account SAS can span several services and allow service-level operations such as listing containers and creating queues. A user delegation SAS applies only to Blob storage, so it cannot create queues.","Domain 2: Shared access signatures",["A user delegation SAS works only for Blob storage, so it cannot create queues.","A service SAS is limited to one service, so a blob container SAS cannot touch Queue storage.","A stored access policy is attached to one queue or container; it cannot span services or list containers.",null],3],["x17",0,2,"A standard requires data at rest in a storage account to be encrypted at two layers: once at the service level and again at the infrastructure level. What should be configured?",["Customer-managed keys in Key Vault, with rotation","Encryption scopes applied to each container in turn","Infrastructure encryption, chosen at account creation","Client-side encryption performed by the application SDK"],2,"Infrastructure encryption adds a second layer of encryption beneath the default service-level encryption and must be enabled when the account is created. Customer-managed keys change who controls the key but still give one layer.","Domain 2: Storage encryption",["Customer-managed keys change key ownership but still give a single layer of service encryption.","Encryption scopes let containers or blobs use different keys but do not add an infrastructure-level layer.",null,"Client-side encryption is done by the app, not the platform's service and infrastructure layers the standard names."],1],["x18",0,2,"The SOC wants Defender for Storage alerts to be ranked higher when the affected blobs contain credit card numbers or other sensitive information types. Which capability does this?",["Sensitive data threat detection","Malware scanning on upload","Activity monitoring only","Blob versioning with soft delete"],0,"Sensitive data threat detection uses sensitive information types to identify which resources hold sensitive data and adds that context to alerts for prioritization. Malware scanning checks uploaded content for malware but does not classify data sensitivity.","Domain 2: Defender for Storage",[null,"Malware scanning checks uploaded files for malware; it does not classify content by sensitive information type.","Activity monitoring detects suspicious access but lacks the sensitivity context used to raise alert priority.","Versioning and soft delete protect against data loss; they provide no alerting or sensitivity classification."],2],["x19",0,2,"One Azure SQL database on a logical server must accept connections from a partner's public IP, but the other databases on the same server must not. What should be created?",["A server-level IP firewall rule for the partner's address","A virtual network rule for the partner's VNet","The 'Allow Azure services and resources' setting on the server","A database-level IP firewall rule in that database"],3,"Database-level IP firewall rules apply only to the database they are created in. A server-level rule would open every database on the logical server to the partner.","Domain 2: Azure SQL firewall rules",["A server-level rule opens every database on the logical server, not just the one the partner needs.","Virtual network rules admit traffic from Azure subnets, not from a partner's public IP address.","That setting lets Azure-hosted traffic in broadly and does nothing for a partner's specific IP.",null],2],["x20",0,2,"Azure SQL uses TDE with a customer-managed TDE protector in Key Vault. During an incident the team removes the server's access to that key. What is the result?",["Nothing changes, because TDE only protects backup files","The databases become inaccessible until key access is restored","The server automatically falls back to a service-managed key","New rows are written unencrypted until the key is restored"],1,"With a customer-managed TDE protector, revoking the server's access to the key makes the databases inaccessible, which is how customers keep control of their data. There is no automatic fallback to a service-managed key.","Domain 2: TDE with customer-managed keys",["TDE encrypts the data and log files and backups; losing the protector affects the live databases too.",null,"There is no automatic fallback; that would defeat the customer's ability to revoke access to their data.","TDE does not switch to writing plaintext; the databases become inaccessible instead."],3],["x21",0,2,"Auditors want Azure SQL audit events queryable with KQL next to other security logs so Microsoft Sentinel can use them. Where should SQL auditing write?",["A storage account with an immutability policy","A Log Analytics workspace","An event hub with no other destination","System tables inside the audited database"],1,"Auditing to a Log Analytics workspace makes events queryable with KQL and available to Sentinel. A storage account keeps the records but they cannot be queried with KQL by Sentinel analytics.","Domain 2: Azure SQL auditing",["Storage keeps audit records but Sentinel analytics and KQL do not query them there.",null,"An event hub streams records elsewhere; on its own it is not queryable with KQL by Sentinel.","Azure SQL auditing does not write to system tables in the audited database as its KQL-ready destination."],1],["x22",0,2,"A new NSG with no custom rules is associated with a subnet. Which inbound traffic does it allow?",["Internet traffic on ports 80 and 443 only","Nothing, including load balancer health probes","RDP and SSH from the internet only","VNet traffic and Azure Load Balancer probes"],3,"The default inbound rules are AllowVnetInBound, AllowAzureLoadBalancerInBound and DenyAllInBound. The deny-all default is below the two allow rules, so probes and VNet traffic are not blocked.","Domain 2: NSG default rules",["No default rule allows internet traffic on web ports; custom rules are needed for that.","AllowAzureLoadBalancerInBound and AllowVnetInBound sit above DenyAllInBound, so not everything is blocked.","Management ports are not opened by default; RDP and SSH from the internet are denied.",null],1],["x23",0,2,"An NSG on a subnet allows inbound TCP 443 from Internet. The VM's NIC also has its own NSG containing only the default rules. Does inbound HTTPS from the internet reach the VM?",["No; both the subnet and NIC NSGs must allow it","Yes; the subnet NSG wins because it is evaluated last","Yes; NIC-level NSGs only filter outbound traffic","No; an NSG cannot be on a subnet and a NIC at once"],0,"For inbound traffic the subnet NSG is evaluated first and then the NIC NSG; both must allow the flow, and the NIC NSG's DenyAllInBound drops it. NIC NSGs filter both directions, so they are not outbound-only.","Domain 2: NSG processing",[null,"For inbound traffic the subnet NSG is evaluated first, and both NSGs must allow the flow.","NIC-level NSGs filter inbound and outbound traffic, not only outbound.","Associating NSGs with both a subnet and a NIC is supported and common."],3],["x24",0,2,"Service endpoints for Storage are enabled on a subnet. Security worries that VMs could copy data to any storage account in the region, not only the company's. What restricts this while keeping service endpoints?",["An outbound NSG rule that uses the regional Storage service tag","A privatelink blob DNS zone linked to the VNet","A service endpoint policy that lists the allowed accounts","Firewall rules on the company's own storage accounts"],2,"Service endpoint policies limit service endpoint traffic from a subnet to specific storage accounts, which addresses exfiltration. The Storage service tag covers every account in the region, including an attacker's.","Domain 2: Service endpoints",["The Storage service tag matches every account in the region, including ones controlled by an attacker.","A privatelink DNS zone is for private endpoints and does not restrict service endpoint destinations.",null,"Firewalls on your own accounts control who reaches them, not where your VMs can send data."],3],["x25",0,2,"Workloads may reach only a partner API hostname and Windows Update over HTTPS; everything else outbound must be denied. Azure Firewall Standard is in place. Which rule type should be used?",["DNAT rules","Network rules listing the partner's IP addresses","NSG rules with service tags","Application rules with target FQDNs"],3,"Application rules filter outbound HTTP/S by fully qualified domain name, and FQDN tags cover services like Windows Update. Network rules work on IPs and ports, which break when the partner's addresses change.","Domain 2: Azure Firewall rules",["DNAT rules translate inbound traffic to internal hosts; they do not filter outbound destinations.","Network rules rely on IP addresses, which break when the partner's or Microsoft's IPs change.","NSGs cannot filter by hostname and are not part of Azure Firewall's rule set.",null],2],["x26",0,2,"A central team wants baseline Azure Firewall rules enforced on every regional firewall, while regional teams add their own local rules. What should be used?",["Classic rules on each firewall, copied by a nightly script","One NSG applied to every AzureFirewallSubnet in each region","A parent firewall policy that child policies inherit","A Virtual Network Manager connectivity configuration"],2,"Firewall policies support a parent-child hierarchy in Firewall Manager, so child policies inherit the central baseline and add local rules. Copying classic rules by script is fragile and has no inheritance.","Domain 2: Firewall Manager",["Script-copied classic rules drift, have no inheritance, and classic rules are not managed by Firewall Manager.","NSGs are not supported on AzureFirewallSubnet and could not provide policy inheritance anyway.",null,"Connectivity configurations build network topology such as hub-and-spoke; they do not hold firewall rules."],2],["x27",0,2,"A new WAF policy on Application Gateway is expected to produce false positives. The team wants to log what it would block before enforcing anything. Which mode should be used first?",["Prevention mode","Detection mode","A custom rule with an Allow action for all","Bot protection turned off"],1,"Detection mode evaluates and logs matches without blocking, so rules can be tuned before switching to prevention. Prevention mode blocks immediately, which is exactly what the team wants to avoid for now.","Domain 2: Web Application Firewall",["Prevention mode blocks matching requests immediately, which risks breaking legitimate traffic before tuning.",null,"An allow-everything custom rule bypasses inspection, so nothing useful would be logged for tuning.","Turning off bot protection reduces coverage and does not produce a log of what would be blocked."],1],["x28",0,2,"A WAF in prevention mode blocks legitimate requests because a form field named 'comment' often contains SQL-like text. Security wants the rest of the rule set to stay active. What is the best action?",["Add an exclusion for that request field","Switch the whole policy to detection mode","Disable the SQL injection rule group entirely","Remove the WAF and rely on NSGs"],0,"A WAF exclusion skips inspection of that one field while every rule still inspects the rest of the request. Disabling the SQL injection rule group would remove protection from every field and page.","Domain 2: WAF tuning",[null,"Detection mode stops blocking everything, removing protection across the whole application.","Disabling the whole rule group removes SQL injection protection from every other field and page.","NSGs work at layers 3 and 4 and cannot inspect request content for attacks."],2],["x29",0,2,"A login page behind Azure Front Door is hit by credential stuffing from many IPs, mostly in countries where the company has no users. Which WAF feature addresses this?",["Custom rules using geo-match and rate limiting","The managed default rule set, left at its default settings","Azure DDoS Network Protection on the origin's virtual network","Private Link between Front Door and the origin app"],0,"WAF custom rules can match on geography and apply rate limits per client, throttling repeated login attempts. DDoS Protection targets volumetric network attacks, not low-rate application-layer login abuse.","Domain 2: WAF on Front Door",[null,"Managed rules detect exploit patterns like SQL injection but do not throttle repeated valid-looking logins.","DDoS Network Protection handles volumetric network floods, not low-rate application-layer login abuse.","Private Link secures the path to the origin but does not filter or rate-limit client requests at the edge."],2],["x30",0,2,"Remote administrators need point-to-site VPN access to a VNet, and sign-in must enforce Conditional Access and MFA. Which authentication type fits?",["Certificate authentication using a self-signed root cert","Entra ID authentication with the Azure VPN Client","RADIUS authentication with only a shared secret","A pre-shared key configured for IKEv2 clients"],1,"Point-to-site VPN with Microsoft Entra ID authentication uses the Azure VPN Client, so Conditional Access and MFA apply to the connection. Certificate authentication proves the device holds a certificate but does not evaluate Conditional Access.","Domain 2: Point-to-site VPN",["Certificate authentication proves the device holds a certificate but does not evaluate Conditional Access.",null,"RADIUS with only a shared secret does not bring Entra Conditional Access into the sign-in.","Point-to-site does not use pre-shared keys for users, and a PSK would not enforce MFA or Conditional Access."],2],["x31",0,2,"A VM's NIC is covered by NSGs at both subnet and NIC level, and some rules use service tags and ASGs. The engineer wants one aggregated list of the rules actually applied, with service tags expanded to prefixes. Which tool fits?",["IP flow verify","Connection monitor","Effective security rules","NSG diagnostic settings"],2,"Effective security rules show the combined rules from all NSGs applied to a NIC and expand service tags into address prefixes. IP flow verify only tests one specific packet and names the rule that matched.","Domain 2: Network Watcher",["IP flow verify tests one packet's 5-tuple and names the matching rule; it does not list all applied rules.","Connection monitor tracks reachability and latency over time, not the aggregated NSG rule set.",null,"Diagnostic settings send NSG logs elsewhere; they do not show the merged effective rules for a NIC."],3],["x32",0,3,"A Foundry app summarizes emails and web pages for users. Attackers hide instructions inside those documents to make the model leak data. Which Azure AI Content Safety feature targets this?",["Groundedness detection","Protected material detection for code and text","Hate and violence severity filters","Prompt Shields for indirect (document) attacks"],3,"Prompt Shields detects both direct jailbreak attempts and indirect attacks embedded in documents or other grounding content. Groundedness detection checks whether answers are supported by sources, not whether sources carry injected instructions.","Domain 3: Prompt Shields",["Groundedness detection checks whether answers match source material, not whether sources carry hidden instructions.","Protected material detection looks for copyrighted text or code in outputs, not injected instructions.","Harm category filters score hateful or violent content, not instructions hidden in documents.",null],3],["x33",0,3,"API Management fronts an Azure OpenAI resource. The team wants APIM to authenticate to the model with no API key stored anywhere. What should be done?",["Store the OpenAI API key in an APIM named value, marked secret","Forward each caller's APIM subscription key to the backend","Give APIM a managed identity with an OpenAI data-plane role","Turn off key authentication and allow anonymous calls"],2,"APIM can use its managed identity to get Entra ID tokens for the backend when that identity holds a role such as Cognitive Services OpenAI User. A secret named value still stores the API key, which the requirement forbids.","Domain 3: AI Gateway",["A named value still stores the API key, so a secret still exists, even if it is marked secret.","APIM subscription keys are gateway credentials and are not accepted by the Azure OpenAI backend.",null,"Allowing anonymous calls removes authentication altogether, which is the opposite of the goal."],2],["x34",0,3,"The team wants per-consumer token usage metrics from an AI Gateway in API Management so model costs can be charged back to each app. Which capability fits?",["A policy that emits token metrics per subscription","A rate-limit policy that counts HTTP calls per IP address","Defender for AI services threat protection alerts","VNet flow logs on the API Management subnet"],0,"APIM's token metric policies emit token counts to Application Insights with dimensions such as subscription ID, enabling chargeback. A call-count rate limit ignores how many tokens each call consumed.","Domain 3: AI Gateway",[null,"Counting HTTP calls ignores how many tokens each call consumed, so it cannot support token-based chargeback.","Defender for AI services detects threats like jailbreak attempts; it does not report token usage per consumer.","VNet flow logs show network flows, not model token consumption per app."],3],["x35",0,3,"An organization is deploying many AI agents that call Microsoft Graph. Security wants each agent to be a distinct identity it can inventory, apply Conditional Access to and disable individually, instead of agents sharing one app secret. What should be used?",["One shared service principal with a long-lived client secret","A licensed user account with a mailbox for every agent","A guest invitation sent to each agent vendor","Microsoft Entra Agent ID identities for each agent"],3,"Microsoft Entra Agent ID gives agents their own identities that can be inventoried, governed by Conditional Access and disabled one by one. A shared service principal makes it impossible to tell agents apart or cut off just one.","Domain 3: Microsoft Entra Agent ID",["A shared service principal cannot distinguish agents, and one leaked secret compromises all of them.","User accounts for agents mix non-human workloads with people and add licences without agent-specific governance.","Guest invitations are for external people, not for identities representing automated agents.",null],2],["x36",0,3,"Managed disks must be encrypted with keys the company controls in Key Vault, and no extension may run inside the guest. What is required?",["Azure Disk Encryption with a key encryption key","A disk encryption set that points to the key","Trusted launch with secure boot and a virtual TPM","An Always Encrypted column master key in the vault"],1,"Server-side encryption with customer-managed keys uses a disk encryption set that references the Key Vault key, and runs in the platform, not the guest. Azure Disk Encryption relies on an extension using BitLocker or DM-Crypt inside the VM.","Domain 3: Disk encryption",["Azure Disk Encryption runs an extension in the guest to use BitLocker or DM-Crypt, which is not allowed here.",null,"Trusted launch protects the boot chain; it does not encrypt disks with customer-managed keys.","Always Encrypted protects columns in SQL databases, not VM managed disks."],2],["x37",0,3,"Trusted launch VMs are deployed, and the team wants Defender for Cloud to report when a VM's boot measurements fail attestation. What must be present on the VMs?",["The Azure Disk Encryption extension","The guest attestation extension","Just-in-time VM access on the VM","The Dependency agent for VM insights"],1,"Boot integrity monitoring relies on the guest attestation extension to attest boot measurements and surface failures in Defender for Cloud. Azure Disk Encryption protects data at rest and plays no part in boot attestation.","Domain 3: Trusted launch",["Disk encryption protects data at rest and has nothing to do with attesting boot measurements.",null,"JIT controls management port access; it does not report on boot integrity.","The Dependency agent maps processes and connections for VM insights, not boot attestation."],3],["x38",0,3,"A user requests just-in-time access to a VM for three hours from their current IP. What does Defender for Cloud actually change?",["It gives the user Virtual Machine Contributor for 3 hours","It deploys a temporary Azure Bastion host into the VNet","It turns off the Windows Firewall inside the guest OS","It adds a temporary NSG allow rule for the source IP"],3,"JIT adds a time-limited allow rule for the requested port and source IP to the NSG (or Azure Firewall), then removes it afterwards. It does not grant RBAC roles; the requester already needs permission to request access.","Domain 3: Just-in-time VM access",["JIT does not grant RBAC roles; the requester already needs permission to request access.","JIT does not deploy Bastion; it opens the management port only for the requester's IP.","JIT works at the NSG or Azure Firewall layer and does not change the guest OS firewall.",null],2],["x39",0,3,"A company only needs Microsoft Defender for Endpoint protection on its Azure VMs, provisioned through Defender for Cloud, at the lowest Defender for Servers plan. Which plan fits?",["Defender for Servers Plan 1","Defender for Servers Plan 2","Foundational CSPM","Defender CSPM"],0,"Plan 1 includes the Defender for Endpoint integration, so it meets the need at the lower tier. Plan 2 adds features such as file integrity monitoring and JIT that were not asked for.","Domain 3: Defender for Servers",[null,"Plan 2 adds extras such as file integrity monitoring that were not required, so it is not the lowest fit.","Foundational CSPM is free posture management and does not include Defender for Endpoint protection.","Defender CSPM adds advanced posture features like attack paths, not endpoint protection for servers."],2],["x40",0,3,"Defender for Cloud should find software vulnerabilities and secrets on VMs without installing any agent, by analyzing disk snapshots. Which capability does this?",["Just-in-time VM access","File integrity monitoring","Agentless machine scanning","Azure Update Manager assessments"],2,"Agentless machine scanning takes snapshots of VM disks and analyzes them out of band for vulnerabilities and secrets. Update Manager assesses missing OS patches, not secrets or broad software vulnerabilities from snapshots.","Domain 3: Defender for Servers",["JIT limits management port exposure and does not scan disks for vulnerabilities or secrets.","File integrity monitoring tracks changes to files and registry, not vulnerabilities found from snapshots.",null,"Update Manager assesses and schedules OS patches; it does not scan for secrets on disks."],1],["x41",0,3,"An AKS cluster uses Entra ID integration, but anyone allowed to run 'az aks get-credentials --admin' can still obtain a static, non-Entra admin kubeconfig. What should be done?",["Enable a private cluster","Enable Defender for Containers on the cluster","Apply a Kubernetes network policy","Disable local accounts on the cluster"],3,"Disabling local accounts removes the static cluster-admin credential so every access goes through Entra ID. A private cluster hides the API server from the internet but still lets anyone on the network use the admin kubeconfig.","Domain 3: AKS security",["A private cluster limits network reachability but anyone on the network can still use the static admin kubeconfig.","Defender for Containers alerts on threats but does not remove the local admin credential.","Network policies control pod-to-pod traffic, not how users authenticate to the API server.",null],3],["x42",0,3,"Developers must not be able to run privileged containers on AKS; such deployments should be rejected at admission time. What should be used?",["Kubernetes network policies in each namespace","Defender for Containers runtime threat alerts","The Azure Policy add-on with a Deny-effect policy","The Azure Kubernetes Service RBAC Reader role"],2,"The Azure Policy add-on for AKS enforces policies through an admission controller, rejecting privileged pods before they run. Defender for Containers alerts on runtime threats but does not block the deployment.","Domain 3: Azure Policy for AKS",["Network policies control pod traffic; they cannot stop a privileged pod from being created.","Runtime alerts fire after the container is running rather than blocking the deployment at admission.",null,"An RBAC Reader role limits one user's actions but does not enforce a rule on everyone's pod specs."],2],["x43",0,3,"A team wants to add a private endpoint to its Azure Container Registry and disable public access, but the portal does not offer these network settings. What is the most likely reason?",["The admin user is still enabled","The registry is not on the Premium tier","Content trust is not enabled","No identity has AcrPull on it"],1,"Private endpoints and network access rules for Azure Container Registry require the Premium service tier. The admin user is a credential concern and does not affect which network features are available.","Domain 3: Container registry security",["The admin user is a credential setting and has no bearing on which network features the tier offers.",null,"Content trust concerns image signing, not the availability of private endpoints.","Role assignments control who can pull images, not whether network settings are available."],3],["x44",0,3,"An App Service app must call an Azure SQL private endpoint that lives in a VNet. Which feature gives the app outbound access into the VNet?",["VNet integration","A private endpoint on the app","Access restrictions","Hybrid Connection Manager"],0,"VNet integration routes the app's outbound traffic into the VNet so it can reach private endpoints. A private endpoint on the app handles inbound traffic to the app, not its outbound calls.","Domain 3: App Service networking",[null,"A private endpoint on the app provides private inbound access to the app, not outbound access from it.","Access restrictions filter who can call the app; they do not route the app's outbound traffic.","Hybrid Connections reach specific host and port endpoints via a relay agent, not a VNet private endpoint natively."],2],["x45",0,3,"A security review finds an App Service app still accepts deployments over plain FTP using publishing credentials. What should be changed?",["Set FTP to disabled or FTPS only","Turn on HTTPS only for site traffic","Raise the minimum inbound TLS version","Add a Key Vault reference for the password"],0,"The FTP state setting controls whether plain FTP, FTPS only or no FTP is allowed for deployment. HTTPS only redirects web traffic from HTTP to HTTPS and has no effect on the FTP endpoint.","Domain 3: App Service hardening",[null,"HTTPS only redirects web requests from HTTP to HTTPS and does not affect the FTP deployment endpoint.","Minimum inbound TLS applies to HTTPS site traffic and does not stop plain FTP.","Key Vault references protect app settings; they do not change how deployments authenticate over FTP."],2],["x46",0,3,"A Function app's Key Vault reference shows an error status. The vault uses the Azure RBAC permission model and the app has a system-assigned managed identity. What is most likely missing?",["A client secret stored in the app registration","Key Vault Secrets User for the app's identity","Purge protection enabled on the target vault","A CanNotDelete lock on the Key Vault resource"],1,"Key Vault references resolve using the app's managed identity, which needs a data-plane role such as Key Vault Secrets User on the vault or secret. A client secret is unnecessary because the managed identity replaces it.","Domain 3: Key Vault references",["A managed identity replaces client secrets, so a secret in the app registration is not what is missing.",null,"Purge protection prevents permanent deletion and does not affect whether the app can read secrets.","A delete lock protects the vault resource but grants no data-plane access to anyone."],3],["x47",0,4,"An engineer wants to ask ad hoc questions such as 'which internet-exposed VMs have high-severity vulnerabilities and permissions to a Key Vault?' and save the query as a template. Which feature fits?",["Workflow automation with a Logic App trigger","The regulatory compliance dashboard view","The Defender CSPM cloud security explorer","Secure score over time in the workbook"],2,"Cloud security explorer runs graph-based queries across resources, exposure, vulnerabilities and permissions, and queries can be saved as templates. Workflow automation reacts to alerts or recommendations; it does not answer ad hoc questions.","Domain 4: Defender CSPM",["Workflow automation runs Logic Apps on alerts or recommendations; it does not answer ad hoc graph questions.","The compliance dashboard maps controls to standards but cannot query exposure plus vulnerability plus permissions.",null,"Secure score history shows trends, not relationships between exposure, vulnerabilities and identities."],2],["x48",0,4,"A recommendation flags a storage account that is protected by a compensating control the auditors accepted. The team wants it out of secure score with a documented reason and an expiry. What should be done?",["Remove the storage account from Defender's asset inventory","Disable the Defender for Storage plan for the subscription","Create a governance rule assigning it to the owner","Create an exemption for that resource as mitigated"],3,"Exemptions remove a resource from a recommendation's evaluation with a category such as mitigated or waiver, a justification and an optional expiry. A governance rule tracks remediation ownership but keeps counting the resource as unhealthy.","Domain 4: Secure score and recommendations",["Removing it from inventory is not how exemptions work and would hide the resource from all posture data.","Disabling a workload protection plan removes threat detection and does not document an accepted mitigation.","Governance rules assign ownership and due dates but keep the resource counted as unhealthy.",null],3],["x49",0,4,"Defender for Cloud alerts and recommendations must be streamed continuously to an event hub so a third-party SIEM can consume them. What should be configured?",["Workflow automation","Governance rules","Continuous export","Cloud security explorer"],2,"Continuous export streams alerts, recommendations and secure score data to a Log Analytics workspace or an event hub. Workflow automation runs Logic Apps on triggers but is not a bulk streaming feed for a SIEM.","Domain 4: Defender for Cloud",["Workflow automation triggers Logic Apps per event; it is not designed as a continuous bulk feed to a SIEM.","Governance rules assign owners and due dates to recommendations and do not export data.",null,"Cloud security explorer runs interactive queries in the portal and does not stream data out."],1],["x50",0,4,"Defender for Servers repeatedly raises the same alert for an approved internal vulnerability scanner. The team wants these alerts dismissed automatically, but only for that scanner's host. What fits?",["An alert suppression rule scoped to that entity","Disable Defender for Servers on the subscription","An exemption on the related recommendation","A Sentinel automation rule that closes all incidents of that alert type from any host"],0,"Alert suppression rules dismiss matching alerts using criteria such as the alert type and specific entities, so only the scanner's alerts are hidden. Closing every incident of that type from any host would also hide real attacks.","Domain 4: Workload protection alerts",[null,"Disabling the plan removes protection from every server, not just the scanner's alerts.","Recommendation exemptions affect posture findings, not security alerts from threat detection.","Closing every incident of that type from any host would also silence genuine attacks on other machines."],2],["x51",0,4,"A multinational must keep EU security logs stored in the EU for data residency, while one SOC investigates incidents across all regions. Which Microsoft Sentinel design fits best?",["One workspace in a US region holding all logs","Delete EU logs after 30 days to reduce exposure","Archive EU logs to storage and skip Sentinel there","Regional workspaces, queried across from one SOC"],3,"Separate workspaces in each required region keep data where it must reside, and Sentinel supports cross-workspace queries and incident management. A single US workspace is simpler but violates the residency requirement.","Domain 4: Sentinel workspace design",["A single US workspace is simpler but stores EU data outside the EU, breaking residency.","Deleting logs early destroys evidence and does not address where the data is stored.","Skipping Sentinel for EU logs leaves those regions without detection and incident handling.",null],3],["x52",0,4,"Windows security events arrive in Sentinel through the Azure Monitor Agent, but one noisy event ID doubles ingestion cost and is never used. The team wants to stop paying for it. What should be done?",["An automation rule that closes related incidents","Filter it in the data collection rule","A watchlist of noisy event IDs used in queries","A retention policy of seven days on the table"],1,"Data collection rules can filter events with XPath queries or transformations before ingestion, so the event is never billed. Automation rules act on incidents after ingestion and do nothing for cost.","Domain 4: Data collection rules",["Automation rules act on incidents after the data is ingested, so the cost is already incurred.",null,"A watchlist can exclude events in queries, but the data is still ingested and billed.","Short retention reduces storage but the ingestion charge, which is the main cost, still applies."],2],["x53",0,4,"Sentinel receives Defender for Cloud alerts through a data connector, not through Defender XDR. The SOC wants an incident created automatically for each high-severity alert. Which rule type is designed for this?",["An anomaly rule","A Microsoft Security rule","A near-real-time rule","A threat intelligence matching rule"],1,"Microsoft Security rules create incidents from alerts generated by Microsoft security products, filtered by severity. NRT rules run custom KQL queries on raw data rather than turning product alerts into incidents.","Domain 4: Sentinel analytics rules",["Anomaly rules produce ML-based anomalies from raw data and do not turn product alerts into incidents.",null,"NRT rules run custom KQL on raw logs; they are not the built-in way to create incidents from product alerts.","TI matching compares indicators with logs; it does not create incidents from Defender for Cloud alerts."],2],["x54",0,4,"The SOC wants to spot users whose data access is unusual compared with their own baseline, using built-in machine learning that analysts can tune without writing ML code. What should be used?",["A scheduled rule with a fixed threshold","A near-real-time rule on AuditLogs","A watchlist of normal user volumes","Built-in anomaly analytics rules"],3,"Anomaly rules use built-in machine learning baselines with adjustable parameters and no ML coding. A fixed-threshold scheduled rule applies one number to everyone and ignores each user's own behaviour.","Domain 4: Sentinel anomaly rules",["A fixed threshold applies one number to all users and ignores each user's normal behaviour.","NRT rules run quickly but still rely on static logic you write, not learned baselines.","A watchlist is a static reference list and cannot learn or adjust baselines per user.",null],2],["x55",0,4,"The SOC imports IP and domain indicators from a threat intelligence feed into Sentinel and wants alerts whenever they appear in firewall and DNS logs. What should be enabled?",["A threat intelligence matching analytics rule","A workbook that charts indicators by type","A Security Copilot promptbook for indicators","A transformation in the TI data collection rule"],0,"Threat intelligence matching analytics compare indicators against log data and generate alerts on hits. A workbook only visualizes the indicators and never alerts on matches in logs.","Domain 4: Threat intelligence",[null,"A workbook visualizes indicators but does not alert when they appear in logs.","Promptbooks run Copilot prompts on demand; they do not continuously match indicators against logs.","A transformation changes data at ingestion but does not compare indicators against firewall and DNS logs."],2],["x56",0,4,"Which KQL operator adds a calculated column to each row while keeping all existing columns?",["project","summarize","extend","where"],2,"extend adds new computed columns and keeps the rest of the row. project can also compute columns but outputs only the columns it lists, dropping all others.","Domain 4: KQL basics",["project keeps only the columns it names, so the other existing columns are dropped.","summarize aggregates rows into groups and discards the per-row detail.",null,"where filters rows by a condition and does not add any columns."],1],["x57",0,4,"Verbose network logs are moved from the Analytics tier to a lower-cost long-term tier such as the Sentinel data lake. What trade-off should the engineer expect?",["The logs are deleted automatically after 24 hours","The logs can no longer be queried by anyone, even with KQL","Their data is sent to Microsoft Purview instead","Lower cost, but more limited query and rule support"],3,"Lower-cost tiers are cheaper for long retention but offer slower, more limited querying and are not used directly by standard real-time analytics. The data can still be queried, for example with KQL jobs, so it is not unreachable.","Domain 4: Log tiers and retention",["Long-term tiers are meant for extended retention, not deletion after a day.","The data is still queryable, for example through KQL queries or jobs, just with more limits.","Moving tiers keeps the data in Sentinel's storage; it is not transferred to Purview.",null],3],["x58",0,4,"An analyst in the Microsoft Defender portal wants Security Copilot to summarize an incident right on the incident page, without opening a separate Copilot portal. Which experience is this?",["The standalone Security Copilot portal","A promptbook run from the standalone portal","The embedded Copilot experience in Defender","A Microsoft Sentinel workbook"],2,"Embedded experiences put Security Copilot inside products such as the Defender portal, for example incident summaries on the incident page. The standalone portal is a separate site, which the analyst wants to avoid.","Domain 4: Security Copilot experiences",["The standalone portal is a separate site, which the analyst specifically wants to avoid.","Promptbooks run in the standalone experience, so they still require leaving the incident page.",null,"A workbook is a reporting dashboard and does not provide Copilot summaries on the incident page."],1],["x59",0,4,"The SOC is overwhelmed by user-reported phishing and wants a Security Copilot capability that autonomously triages submissions, classifies them and explains each verdict for analyst review. What fits?",["A watchlist of users who report phishing","A Security Copilot agent for phishing triage","An Exchange transport rule that deletes reports","A daily Purview Audit search of mailbox actions"],1,"Security Copilot agents perform tasks like phishing triage autonomously and show their reasoning so analysts can review and give feedback. A transport rule that deletes reports would discard evidence without any analysis.","Domain 4: Security Copilot agents",["A list of reporters does nothing to analyze or classify the submitted messages.",null,"Deleting reports discards evidence and gives analysts no verdict or explanation to review.","An audit search shows mailbox activity but does not triage phishing submissions or explain a verdict."],2],["x60",0,4,"SOC leadership wants an interactive Sentinel dashboard showing incident trends, mean time to close and top alert sources. What should be built?",["A workbook","A playbook","A watchlist","A hunting bookmark"],0,"Workbooks provide interactive visual reports over Sentinel data. A playbook is a Logic Apps workflow that automates response actions; it produces no dashboard.","Domain 4: Sentinel workbooks",[null,"A playbook automates response actions through Logic Apps and produces no interactive dashboard.","A watchlist is a reference list for queries, not a visual report.","Bookmarks save interesting hunting results and do not present trends or metrics."],1]]);
